00:38:46.498 --> 00:38:50.200
you know, some content around what Undo is

1043
00:38:50.200 --> 00:38:52.397
and what it means to the fitness world

1044
00:38:52.397 --> 00:38:53.713
and the wellness movement.

1045
00:38:54.580 --> 00:38:55.552
So yeah, I had a meeting.

1046
00:38:55.552 --> 00:38:58.314
I had to look over like the final, you know,

1047
00:38:58.314 --> 00:39:00.170
PDF of the issue.

1048
00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:01.003
Sign on that-

1049
00:39:01.003 --> 00:39:02.438
So, when's it gonna drop?

1050
00:39:02.438 --> 00:39:05.020
July, it should be by July 31st.

1051
00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:06.866
So, we're literally sending it to print today.

1052
00:39:06.866 --> 00:39:08.400
And will it be in Urban Outfitter stores?

1053
00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:10.395
Yeah, it'll be in Urban stores.

1054
00:39:10.395 --> 00:39:12.521
It will be, we have a bunch of distributors worldwide,

1055
00:39:12.521 --> 00:39:16.357
a lot of like small sportswear shops,

1056
00:39:16.357 --> 00:39:18.475
like Black Rainbow in Paris, you know,

1057
00:39:18.475 --> 00:39:21.230
little places, really just through friends,

1058
00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:23.090
and we're just like, you wanna carry our magazine?

1059
00:39:23.090 --> 00:39:25.540
(everyone laughs)

1060
00:39:25.540 --> 00:39:27.625
So, it's just, I mean, the hustle is real, man.

1061
00:39:27.625 --> 00:39:29.000
The hustle is real.

1062
00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:30.582
And it's honestly not to make,

1063
00:39:30.582 --> 00:39:33.130
I mean, we're gonna make some money,

1064
00:39:33.130 --> 00:39:35.810
but we're donating a large part of the money we make

1065
00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:36.966
actually to Girls Gotta Run,

1066
00:39:36.966 --> 00:39:41.966
which is a nonprofit in Ethiopia that provides young girls

1067
00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:45.905
from like nine to 18 with running gear and training camps,

1068
00:39:45.905 --> 00:39:49.330
and yeah, we officially partner with them.

1069
00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:53.690
And yeah, it's so much more than like a dollar.

1070
00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:55.020
Right, right, right.

1071
00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:56.300
It's service.

1072
00:39:56.300 --> 00:39:57.133
For sure.

1073
00:39:57.133 --> 00:39:58.089
It's a movement.

1074
00:39:58.089 --> 00:40:03.089
It's a movement that nourishes me so deeply, I...

1075
00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:08.968
To be even a participant in the conversation,

1076
00:40:08.968 --> 00:40:11.864
let alone like call myself someone

1077
00:40:11.864 --> 00:40:15.632
who's pushing the conversation forward, is a real honor.

1078
00:40:15.632 --> 00:40:17.620
And thankfully social media makes it possible.

1079
00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:21.121
You know, we have little things like hashtag campaigns.

1080
00:40:21.121 --> 00:40:24.220
And now when I search Undo Ordinary,

1081
00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:25.215
I'm like not even a year ago,

1082
00:40:25.215 --> 00:40:27.608
this was just like a concept in my mind.

1083
00:40:27.608 --> 00:40:29.408
And now I have people I'm connected with

1084
00:40:29.408 --> 00:40:31.391
from Russia to India.

1085
00:40:31.391 --> 00:40:36.120
I mean, they're writing to me the most like humbling,

1086
00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:38.360
profound notes.

1087
00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:41.070
And I'm like, A, you did that,

1088
00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:42.543
B, wow.

1089
00:40:43.390 --> 00:40:45.040
There's nothing I can really say.

1090
00:40:46.543 --> 00:40:48.760
So, yeah, typical day, that,

1091
00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:50.965
and then I had a photo shoot.

1092
00:40:50.965 --> 00:40:52.377
And I went from there to a photo shoot,

1093
00:40:52.377 --> 00:40:54.772
answered some emails, tweets, Instagram.

1094
00:40:54.772 --> 00:40:57.100
God, this rabbit hole of social media.

1095
00:40:57.100 --> 00:40:59.967
You're your own walking reality show really.

1096
00:40:59.967 --> 00:41:01.306
(Robin laughs)

1097
00:41:01.306 --> 00:41:02.639
I mean, they're so,

1098
00:41:02.639 --> 00:41:05.143
you know, probably had a fight with my boyfriend,

1099
00:41:06.048 --> 00:41:07.800
and then I taught three more classes.

1100
00:41:07.800 --> 00:41:09.030
So, yeah, I taught six classes.

1101
00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:10.730
Oh, and then I went on my own run.

1102
00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:13.180
Yeah, so it's like,

1103
00:41:13.180 --> 00:41:16.023
normally it's like five to six classes a day,

1104
00:41:16.023 --> 00:41:20.510
six to 10 miles and business.

1105
00:41:20.510 --> 00:41:21.430
Hustle.

1106
00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:22.263
Filling it in.

1107
00:41:22.263 --> 00:41:23.930
I bet every day's pretty much different

1108
00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:26.320
except you're probably teaching classes in some respect

1109
00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:27.153
or another.

1110
00:41:27.153 --> 00:41:28.280
Yeah, I teach 22 classes a week.

1111
00:41:28.280 --> 00:41:29.780
Wow.

1112
00:41:29.780 --> 00:41:31.450
Yeah, what part of town do you live in?

1113
00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:32.860
I live downtown.

1114
00:41:32.860 --> 00:41:35.258
I wouldn't go above Houseton Street if I had a choice.

1115
00:41:35.258 --> 00:41:36.492
(Robin laughs)

1116
00:41:36.492 --> 00:41:37.839
So this is of privilege.

1117
00:41:37.839 --> 00:41:41.480
This is luxury right here to go above.

1118
00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:43.401
I'd actually much prefer to be downtown,

1119
00:41:43.401 --> 00:41:45.870
but I also like running in the park.

1120
00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:47.140
Yeah, I mean, listen,

1121
00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:49.422
like I get the draw to nature,

1122
00:41:49.422 --> 00:41:53.120
and I showed up to my first trail race,

1123
00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:56.570
like my first trail race actually ever,

1124
00:41:56.570 --> 00:41:59.036
literally ever running on a trail was a 50 miler.

1125
00:41:59.036 --> 00:41:59.869
(Robin laughs)

1126
00:41:59.869 --> 00:42:02.364
I went to REI, and I was like, oh, I need some shoes to run

1127
00:42:02.364 --> 00:42:03.690
in the wilderness.

1128
00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:05.150
So, thankfully I got something with a rock plate

1129
00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:07.720
'cause oh my God, it was crazy.

1130
00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:09.491
So, I get it, I really do.

1131
00:42:09.491 --> 00:42:13.270
Like, I nourish that in me, but when I'm in New York,

1132
00:42:13.270 --> 00:42:16.970
like I feel truly like I'm pushing the pulse of the city

1133
00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:20.497
when I run the bridges, the streets, Brooklyn, like-

1134
00:42:20.497 --> 00:42:21.955
It's urban trail running.

1135
00:42:21.955 --> 00:42:22.913
It is.

1136
00:42:22.913 --> 00:42:24.020
It is.

1137
00:42:24.020 --> 00:42:26.966
It's like, can I spot like a new graffiti piece?

1138
00:42:26.966 --> 00:42:29.340
You know, and I might know the artist,

1139
00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:32.390
you know, that's like, it's a different kind of excavation.

1140
00:42:32.390 --> 00:42:35.210
Right, well, I find, and I like doing that too.

1141
00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:37.430
And when I stay downtown that's exactly what I do

1142
00:42:37.430 --> 00:42:38.263
and I love it.

1143
00:42:38.263 --> 00:42:41.110
And it's like when I'm at home, I'm running trails

1144
00:42:41.110 --> 00:42:43.274
and there's like a kind of an active meditation

1145
00:42:43.274 --> 00:42:44.482
kind of aspect to that.

1146
00:42:44.482 --> 00:42:47.770
But the visual stimulation of running in New York,

1147
00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:49.130
I mean, even when you're running in the park,

1148
00:42:49.130 --> 00:42:50.490
it's just constant people watching.

1149
00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:51.463
You know, it's just fascinating.

1150
00:42:51.463 --> 00:42:56.051
It's so, I always run harder and longer and faster

1151
00:42:56.051 --> 00:42:58.250
in New York City than I do elsewhere

1152
00:42:58.250 --> 00:43:00.630
because it's just, there's so much energy.

1153
00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:02.069
You know, you take that energy on

1154
00:43:02.069 --> 00:43:04.333
and just running through the streets and it's so fun.

1155
00:43:04.333 --> 00:43:05.679
Like, it's my, like it's weird.

1156
00:43:05.679 --> 00:43:08.935
Like, you know, I love trail running,

1157
00:43:08.935 --> 00:43:11.219
but I think New York City is like one of my favorite places

1158
00:43:11.219 --> 00:43:13.960
to run because it's so unique in that regard.

1159
00:43:13.960 --> 00:43:16.010
And you never run out of interesting things to see.

1160
00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:18.837
And you're just constantly like being enriched

1161
00:43:18.837 --> 00:43:22.087
by the experience of just the visual palette

1162
00:43:22.087 --> 00:43:24.061
of what this city has to offer.

1163
00:43:24.061 --> 00:43:24.984
It's like a game of Frogger.

1164
00:43:24.984 --> 00:43:25.817
It is.

1165
00:43:25.817 --> 00:43:26.738
It's a video game.

1166
00:43:26.738 --> 00:43:28.010
It's totally like a video game.

1167
00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:29.371
Yeah, I'm like running,

1168
00:43:29.371 --> 00:43:32.190
like trying not to get run over by taxi cabs.

1169
00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:34.470
Same thing, like biking through the city. I mean, I love it.

1170
00:43:34.470 --> 00:43:35.921
Yeah, and it's not like, oh, there's a stoplight.

1171
00:43:35.921 --> 00:43:36.876
We have to stop.

1172
00:43:36.876 --> 00:43:37.855
Like, damn.

1173
00:43:37.855 --> 00:43:39.670
It's like, no, this is part of it.

1174
00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:42.450
Like, this is part of the game, you know, of doing this.

1175
00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:44.690
So, I wanna hear like a little bit about like,

1176
00:43:44.690 --> 00:43:47.852
'cause it's all about like the urban experience for you

1177
00:43:47.852 --> 00:43:49.120
of running, right?

1178
00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:51.690
And you've created like this cultish, you know,

1179
00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:52.560
movement around this.

1180
00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:55.587
So, like speak to that a little bit.

1181
00:43:55.587 --> 00:43:58.023
I mean, when I moved back from law school,

1182
00:43:58.023 --> 00:43:59.710
I was in law school.

1183
00:43:59.710 --> 00:44:00.910
I was in Philadelphia for law school,

1184
00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:03.810
moved back to New York City, and I was a solo runner,

1185
00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:06.070
and I really didn't know what it felt like

1186
00:44:06.070 --> 00:44:07.270
to be part of a community.

1187
00:44:07.270 --> 00:44:09.380
I'd never been part of team sports.

1188
00:44:09.380 --> 00:44:13.270
And I showed up one day to this downtown running crew

1189
00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:14.179
called Bridge Runners.

1190
00:44:14.179 --> 00:44:17.190
And I was like, oh man, I found like my people,

1191
00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:18.420
I found like my weirdos.

1192
00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:20.463
They're like not traditional athletes.

1193
00:44:20.463 --> 00:44:22.860
Nobody was a former track star.

1194
00:44:22.860 --> 00:44:23.820
I mean, they're like,

1195
00:44:23.820 --> 00:44:26.654
I mean, God, some of them smoke more weed

1196
00:44:26.654 --> 00:44:28.500
than is humanly possible.

1197
00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:30.085
And it's like, I was like, cool.

1198
00:44:30.085 --> 00:44:32.130
I found like my Motley Crew.

1199
00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:37.130
And miles and miles and miles and like bonded by sweat.

1200
00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:38.680
We became a family.

1201
00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:41.360
And then we realized there were little families

1202
00:44:41.360 --> 00:44:42.193
all over the place.

1203
00:44:42.193 --> 00:44:44.510
And now we have like friends who are crews in London

1204
00:44:44.510 --> 00:44:45.343
and Paris.

1205
00:44:45.343 --> 00:44:46.176
And we are just

1206
00:44:46.176 --> 00:44:50.010
like this really rich international community of runners

1207
00:44:50.010 --> 00:44:52.443
who are kind of off the beaten track.

1208
00:44:52.443 --> 00:44:54.401
I mean, it was like, we just-

1209
00:44:54.401 --> 00:44:56.060
You know what it is, like it's-

1210
00:44:56.060 --> 00:44:56.997
So rogue, you know,

1211
00:44:56.997 --> 00:44:58.780
it's like the "Warriors" movie.

1212
00:44:58.780 --> 00:44:59.613
I was gonna say-

1213
00:44:59.613 --> 00:45:00.773
That's what it's like.

1214
00:45:00.773 --> 00:45:03.380
It's like "Warriors" or like "West Side Story."

1215
00:45:03.380 --> 00:45:06.160
I see your Instagram feed and I'm like, it's this gang,

1216
00:45:06.160 --> 00:45:07.470
you know, you're running in New York City.

1217
00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:09.620
I was like, this is like some weird choreographed,

1218
00:45:09.620 --> 00:45:10.660
like "West Side Story."

1219
00:45:10.660 --> 00:45:12.620
We're gonna start snapping,

1220
00:45:12.620 --> 00:45:14.981
you know, snapping choreography.

1221
00:45:14.981 --> 00:45:19.981
Yeah, I mean, but there's this herd...

1222
00:45:20.513 --> 00:45:22.150
Herd mentality in a good way.

1223
00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:24.810
Not in a following, not in a sheepish way,

1224
00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:26.533
but in a way that's like,

1225
00:45:28.310 --> 00:45:30.340
yeah, you're not going anywhere, taxi.

1226
00:45:30.340 --> 00:45:31.471
Like, we're taking this light.

1227
00:45:31.471 --> 00:45:34.944
And we're, it's just, we literally take over the streets.

1228
00:45:34.944 --> 00:45:37.682
I mean, hundreds deep sometimes on a run.

1229
00:45:37.682 --> 00:45:42.580
And we become part of the fabric of New York City

1230
00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:43.580
in a very real way.

1231
00:45:43.580 --> 00:45:45.092
I mean, people are just like, whoa, what was that?

1232
00:45:45.092 --> 00:45:47.000
Like, it's just a force.

1233
00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:48.172
And I never felt that before.

1234
00:45:48.172 --> 00:45:51.906
And then, so from there meeting all these other crews

1235
00:45:51.906 --> 00:45:54.737
and then sort of starting my own movement,

1236
00:45:54.737 --> 00:45:56.870
which has taken it now outside of running

1237
00:45:56.870 --> 00:45:58.110
into other modalities

1238
00:45:58.110 --> 00:45:59.670
and then bringing in the fashion element

1239
00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:01.320
and the lifestyle element,

1240
00:46:01.320 --> 00:46:06.320
and it just sparked, it sparked something like,

1241
00:46:06.594 --> 00:46:08.620
I don't even know where I got this quote,

1242
00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:12.310
some really profound, prolific author, I'm sure.

1243
00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:14.067
But it's, you know, I think it's Rumi.

1244
00:46:14.067 --> 00:46:17.360
"Seeking out people who fan your flames."

1245
00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:19.430
And I'm like, God, I was engulfed.

1246
00:46:19.430 --> 00:46:21.917
And I was so passionate from these experiences

1247
00:46:21.917 --> 00:46:24.390
that it got to the point where I was leading a double life.

1248
00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:27.510
Like, I would just wait all day to make it to the run.

1249
00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:29.500
And thankfully, most of the runs were later at night,

1250
00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:33.120
like total night owls, so I was able to make it

1251
00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:33.953
when I was a lawyer.

1252
00:46:33.953 --> 00:46:35.936
I'm like, I can't do this anymore.

1253
00:46:35.936 --> 00:46:40.353
Like, I've just gotta find a way to pay my rent doing this.

1254
00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:43.010
And somehow I've been able to, you know-

1255
00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:45.010
And there's no path.
No, there's no path.

1256
00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:46.580
There wasn't even anybody I could talk to.

1257
00:46:46.580 --> 00:46:49.906
Like, it wasn't like there was somebody high up

1258
00:46:49.906 --> 00:46:52.716
who I just had to like tap into their knowledge.

1259
00:46:52.716 --> 00:46:57.682
Like, I didn't know anything about this space as a business.

1260
00:46:57.682 --> 00:47:02.337
And the business as a business was sort of was born

1261
00:47:02.337 --> 00:47:04.250
out of these experiences.

1262
00:47:04.250 --> 00:47:06.570
Like, really Bridge Runners is one of the first

1263
00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:09.504
if not the first New York City running crew

1264
00:47:09.504 --> 00:47:12.153
as like that vernacular has now come to be.

1265
00:47:12.153 --> 00:47:15.013
And crew differing from a club

1266
00:47:15.013 --> 00:47:17.411
in that a club is like a training group

1267
00:47:17.411 --> 00:47:21.853
and a crew is like this family, this gang, this,

1268
00:47:22.864 --> 00:47:24.710
it's not what happens on Tuesday night at the run,

1269
00:47:24.710 --> 00:47:26.554
but it's what happens Wednesday morning at the club,

1270
00:47:26.554 --> 00:47:28.750
or Saturday at the Christening.

1271
00:47:28.750 --> 00:47:29.583
You know, it's, yeah, yeah.

1272
00:47:29.583 --> 00:47:30.810
It extends beyond that,

1273
00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:32.500
beyond the run that you're doing, right.

1274
00:47:32.500 --> 00:47:33.333
Absolutely.

1275
00:47:33.333 --> 00:47:34.166
Into your whole,

1276
00:47:34.166 --> 00:47:36.063
like woven into your entire lifestyle.

1277
00:47:36.063 --> 00:47:38.433
Yeah, it's kind of like this,

1278
00:47:40.210 --> 00:47:44.355
this ethos driven training style

1279
00:47:44.355 --> 00:47:46.930
where you're not really doing it.

1280
00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:48.900
You're not necessarily doing it for the finish line.

1281
00:47:48.900 --> 00:47:53.150
You're doing it like to make the family proud.

1282
00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:54.170
I don't know.

1283
00:47:54.170 --> 00:47:55.003
Yeah, I mean,

1284
00:47:55.003 --> 00:47:57.744
I think it's like an analogy would be, you know,

1285
00:47:57.744 --> 00:47:59.408
you're on a diet to lose weight,

1286
00:47:59.408 --> 00:48:01.345
to hit some specific weight, you know,

1287
00:48:01.345 --> 00:48:02.678
but it's not a lifestyle.

1288
00:48:02.678 --> 00:48:04.380
It's not what you live and breathe.

1289
00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:05.213
It's a temporary thing.

1290
00:48:05.213 --> 00:48:08.779
Or training for a race so that you can qualify

1291
00:48:08.779 --> 00:48:11.540
for another race or get your best time.

1292
00:48:11.540 --> 00:48:13.200
Like, it's not really about that.

1293
00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:16.696
It's about the day to day experience of what you're living

1294
00:48:16.696 --> 00:48:21.050
and breathing by being part of this crew, right?

1295
00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:22.710
Yeah, absolutely.

1296
00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:25.512
And I really, I like to lead a life

1297
00:48:25.512 --> 00:48:27.840
where there really is no finish line.

1298
00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:29.000
Like, finish lines are great.

1299
00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:29.833
Like, don't get me wrong,

1300
00:48:29.833 --> 00:48:31.800
I'm definitely competitive with myself.

1301
00:48:31.800 --> 00:48:33.540
And I love a PR, all that jazz.

1302
00:48:33.540 --> 00:48:37.590
But it's kind of like raising the bar

1303
00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:38.950
and then taking it to the next level

1304
00:48:38.950 --> 00:48:41.170
and then harnessing the power of community to do that.

1305
00:48:41.170 --> 00:48:43.210
And I think that is the, for me,

1306
00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:47.220
that's been the perfect recipe for the catalyst

1307
00:48:47.220 --> 00:48:48.053
to my success.

1308
00:48:48.053 --> 00:48:49.720
Right, like raising the bar for the weakest member

1309
00:48:49.720 --> 00:48:50.553
of the crew.

1310
00:48:50.553 --> 00:48:51.617
Yeah, no runner left behind.

1311
00:48:51.617 --> 00:48:55.320
Like, that is like a refrain that you'll always hear.

1312
00:48:55.320 --> 00:48:56.270
'Cause people are always like,

1313
00:48:56.270 --> 00:48:58.700
oh, we see these pictures and you guys look so rad

1314
00:48:58.700 --> 00:49:00.597
and badass and I'm like, you're gonna be fine.

1315
00:49:00.597 --> 00:49:01.820
Like, trust me.

1316
00:49:01.820 --> 00:49:03.013
Like, the fastest runner in the group

1317
00:49:03.013 --> 00:49:05.420
at some point has been the slowest runner.

1318
00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:08.170
Like, it's just the way evolution works.

1319
00:49:08.170 --> 00:49:09.982
But you've also had some badass finish lines.

1320
00:49:09.982 --> 00:49:11.736
Yeah, you know, it's been fun.

1321
00:49:11.736 --> 00:49:13.244
So, let's talk about some of these epic runs

1322
00:49:13.244 --> 00:49:14.661
that you've done.

1323
00:49:15.893 --> 00:49:17.950
God, I love running.

1324
00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:18.783
I just do.

1325
00:49:18.783 --> 00:49:19.780
I just love it.

1326
00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:22.450
I'm like I wanna go run right now.

1327
00:49:23.730 --> 00:49:26.250
Yeah, I think, so I've done some trail races,

1328
00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:27.235
some 50 milers.

1329
00:49:27.235 --> 00:49:29.568
I think the most,

1330
00:49:29.568 --> 00:49:34.568
definitely the most humbling thing I've done, and I,

1331
00:49:34.809 --> 00:49:37.355
you know, I really want, have you run across the country?

1332
00:49:37.355 --> 00:49:39.513
No.
I really wanna run,

1333
00:49:39.513 --> 00:49:42.220
there's something like lodged in the back of my brain

1334
00:49:42.220 --> 00:49:44.450
about that, but that's an aside.

1335
00:49:44.450 --> 00:49:47.880
So, I think last summer I was running across Utah.

1336
00:49:47.880 --> 00:49:49.940
I did five, it was kind of like set up like a stage race.

1337
00:49:49.940 --> 00:49:51.737
I did five marathons in five days

1338
00:49:51.737 --> 00:49:53.940
to raise money for MS Run the US.

1339
00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:55.200
My mom has MS.

1340
00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:57.542
And my very, very first marathon I signed up for

1341
00:49:57.542 --> 00:50:00.476
after a breakup (laughs) and I was like,

1342
00:50:00.476 --> 00:50:02.017
my mom wasn't doing well

1343
00:50:02.017 --> 00:50:04.100
and she was having a lot of issues with her MS.

1344
00:50:04.100 --> 00:50:05.840
And I was like, I gotta do something.

1345
00:50:05.840 --> 00:50:09.050
And so I signed, that's how my first marathon came to be.

1346
00:50:09.050 --> 00:50:10.430
And then from there I was just like,

1347
00:50:10.430 --> 00:50:11.939
okay, I need more and more and more of this.

1348
00:50:11.939 --> 00:50:16.939
But the running across Utah as part of that relay team was,

1349
00:50:20.390 --> 00:50:25.390
I didn't understand until then the mental game

1350
00:50:26.690 --> 00:50:30.120
that was required as an endurance athlete.

1351
00:50:30.120 --> 00:50:31.980
I prepared myself for physical discomfort.

1352
00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:34.760
I did all like the two a days, 100 mile weeks.

1353
00:50:34.760 --> 00:50:36.370
I mean, I've never run more in my life

1354
00:50:36.370 --> 00:50:37.630
than preparing for that.

1355
00:50:37.630 --> 00:50:39.100
And because people were donating,

1356
00:50:39.100 --> 00:50:41.125
I felt even more pressure to like, I have to do it.

1357
00:50:41.125 --> 00:50:43.360
I'm telling people and asking people to give me money,

1358
00:50:43.360 --> 00:50:44.780
I've gotta do this thing.

1359
00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:46.710
So yeah, I started at one point in Utah

1360
00:50:46.710 --> 00:50:48.017
and finished at the other point,

1361
00:50:48.017 --> 00:50:50.590
and then passed on the baton to the next runner.

1362
00:50:50.590 --> 00:50:51.440
So I'm like, you know-

1363
00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:52.633
So it was a relay across America

1364
00:50:52.633 --> 00:50:55.377
where each person got a certain distance

1365
00:50:55.377 --> 00:50:56.580
that they had to cover.

1366
00:50:56.580 --> 00:50:57.413
Yeah, point to point.

1367
00:50:57.413 --> 00:50:59.722
Most people, I actually did the least amount of miles.

1368
00:50:59.722 --> 00:51:04.480
I think some folks did like nine straight marathons or more.

1369
00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:06.765
Who got like Nebraska?

1370
00:51:06.765 --> 00:51:07.740
God, I don't know.

1371
00:51:07.740 --> 00:51:09.649
I don't know how I got Utah, man.

1372
00:51:09.649 --> 00:51:13.940
Like, you know, it's funny 'cause the relay was ending

1373
00:51:13.940 --> 00:51:16.600
right around Labor Day, and I go to Burning Man every year,

1374
00:51:16.600 --> 00:51:17.433
and I was like,

1375
00:51:17.433 --> 00:51:19.090
and I really wanted the New Jersey, New York leg

1376
00:51:19.090 --> 00:51:21.420
'cause then all my crew could come out and whatever.

1377
00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:22.253
And I was like, all right,

1378
00:51:22.253 --> 00:51:24.037
this is gonna be pretty much a solo venture then

1379
00:51:24.037 --> 00:51:25.120
'cause I'm going to Utah.

1380
00:51:25.120 --> 00:51:27.840
I don't know how many people I can get to come with me.

1381
00:51:27.840 --> 00:51:28.910
(Robin laughs)

1382
00:51:28.910 --> 00:51:31.000
No, but it was, I mean,

1383
00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:33.798
you'd think I'd be like taking in the majestic mountains

1384
00:51:33.798 --> 00:51:36.500
and like, I was like, screw that mountain.

1385
00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:37.806
I have to run up it like.

1386
00:51:37.806 --> 00:51:40.511
Thankfully, so there was a documentary film crew there

1387
00:51:40.511 --> 00:51:41.844
capturing the whole thing

1388
00:51:41.844 --> 00:51:44.388
and there's a documentary coming out next year,

1389
00:51:44.388 --> 00:51:46.704
sort of about my journey and my relationship with my mom

1390
00:51:46.704 --> 00:51:48.787
and like the hostage thing

1391
00:51:48.787 --> 00:51:50.780
and just, I mean, I guess like largely

1392
00:51:50.780 --> 00:51:52.929
about my life path so far,

1393
00:51:52.929 --> 00:51:54.883
and they were there to capture it.

1394
00:51:54.883 --> 00:51:56.856
So, I'm really grateful that they have that footage

1395
00:51:56.856 --> 00:51:59.540
'cause now I can, now I just saw the trailer and I'm like,

1396
00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:00.901
ah, that's really beautiful.

1397
00:52:00.901 --> 00:52:02.370
I'm glad I did that.

1398
00:52:02.370 --> 00:52:04.810
But in the moment you're just like so mired

1399
00:52:04.810 --> 00:52:07.660
and like the rawness of the experience and the pain,

1400
00:52:07.660 --> 00:52:08.493
but it was-

1401
00:52:08.493 --> 00:52:09.650
What was the hardest part of that run?

1402
00:52:09.650 --> 00:52:11.928
Mentally, it was just waking up every day

1403
00:52:11.928 --> 00:52:12.860
and doing it again.

1404
00:52:12.860 --> 00:52:13.719
Like, I would wake up

1405
00:52:13.719 --> 00:52:15.533
and I'd be like, no way I'm gonna do that again.

1406
00:52:15.533 --> 00:52:18.377
And really after the fifth, I could have kept going.

1407
00:52:18.377 --> 00:52:20.800
I mean, physically, I was truly fine.

1408
00:52:20.800 --> 00:52:22.610
I'd done plenty of training.

1409
00:52:22.610 --> 00:52:26.918
It was just that little voice inside saying like,

1410
00:52:26.918 --> 00:52:28.400
you can't do this.

1411
00:52:28.400 --> 00:52:32.114
And it was just all like an internal conversation

1412
00:52:32.114 --> 00:52:35.447
that was mind blowing to me

1413
00:52:35.447 --> 00:52:38.580
'cause I'm like I have visualized this and prepared

1414
00:52:38.580 --> 00:52:39.670
and like read all the books

1415
00:52:39.670 --> 00:52:41.596
and it was like, you can't prepare for the moments

1416
00:52:41.596 --> 00:52:44.366
when you're gonna be your worst critic.

1417
00:52:44.366 --> 00:52:48.380
And it was just like standing on the side of the road.

1418
00:52:48.380 --> 00:52:52.260
I had a total like diva Beyonce moment

1419
00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:53.093
on the side of the road,

1420
00:52:53.093 --> 00:52:54.527
there were like 18 wheelers passing by,

1421
00:52:54.527 --> 00:52:56.700
and I was like, I just had a breakdown.

1422
00:52:56.700 --> 00:52:57.533
I was like, I'm unsafe.

1423
00:52:57.533 --> 00:52:59.330
This is like messed up.

1424
00:52:59.330 --> 00:53:01.062
And I'm like, you're stronger than this.

1425
00:53:01.062 --> 00:53:02.270
Like, buck up.

1426
00:53:02.270 --> 00:53:04.190
Like, you almost died at the end of a gun.

1427
00:53:04.190 --> 00:53:06.190
How many marathons in were you when that happened?

1428
00:53:06.190 --> 00:53:07.870
That was on my last day.

1429
00:53:07.870 --> 00:53:09.844
That was my last 10 miles.

1430
00:53:09.844 --> 00:53:11.510
And-
Ready to throw it in.

1431
00:53:11.510 --> 00:53:14.190
No, and it was like totally illogical,

1432
00:53:14.190 --> 00:53:17.399
but it was like in those moments you become a two year old.

1433
00:53:17.399 --> 00:53:19.550
Like, you are just like an infant,

1434
00:53:19.550 --> 00:53:21.086
like and nobody can tell you otherwise.

1435
00:53:21.086 --> 00:53:23.370
And I think I've definitely like, you know,

1436
00:53:23.370 --> 00:53:24.390
I've crewed some friends,

1437
00:53:24.390 --> 00:53:27.110
and I've seen, I've seen that in other runners

1438
00:53:27.110 --> 00:53:28.380
and I'm like, okay, I wasn't alone.

1439
00:53:28.380 --> 00:53:31.230
Like, that was like, I was like hallucinating,

1440
00:53:31.230 --> 00:53:32.445
and, you know, it was just crazy.

1441
00:53:32.445 --> 00:53:33.278
Yeah.

1442
00:53:33.278 --> 00:53:34.270
And that's why they say never have your friends

1443
00:53:34.270 --> 00:53:35.931
or people you care about crew for you

1444
00:53:35.931 --> 00:53:38.740
because you really, you, you know, yeah, yeah, yeah.

1445
00:53:38.740 --> 00:53:39.573
It's an intense.

1446
00:53:39.573 --> 00:53:40.440
You know, you've been there.

1447
00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:41.273
It's an intense-

1448
00:53:41.273 --> 00:53:42.450
It's not pretty, you know what I mean?

1449
00:53:42.450 --> 00:53:43.520
I talk about it in my book.

1450
00:53:43.520 --> 00:53:45.460
It's like you start are behaving in a way

1451
00:53:45.460 --> 00:53:46.503
that you're not proud of.

1452
00:53:46.503 --> 00:53:47.336
No, no.

1453
00:53:47.336 --> 00:53:48.169
And your stripped raw.

1454
00:53:48.169 --> 00:53:49.627
There's nothing there, you know?

1455
00:53:49.627 --> 00:53:53.153
And that's where you really meet your maker.

1456
00:53:53.153 --> 00:53:54.040
Yeah.

1457
00:53:54.040 --> 00:53:55.924
And you're, I mean, you're lacing up and it's like,

1458
00:53:55.924 --> 00:53:57.550
who really, who are you really?

1459
00:53:57.550 --> 00:54:01.503
And I was like, it was not, it was not pretty,

1460
00:54:01.503 --> 00:54:03.031
it was not pretty at the side of that road.

1461
00:54:03.031 --> 00:54:05.460
But to be able to push, but to push through that,

1462
00:54:05.460 --> 00:54:08.630
that's where like you talk about like these, you know,

1463
00:54:08.630 --> 00:54:09.550
empowerment.

1464
00:54:09.550 --> 00:54:11.594
I mean, that moment is the moment,

1465
00:54:11.594 --> 00:54:14.480
like all the other marathons leading up to that was all

1466
00:54:14.480 --> 00:54:15.313
for that.

1467
00:54:15.313 --> 00:54:16.385
Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

1468
00:54:16.385 --> 00:54:20.076
And I had a really hard race in San Diego recently.

1469
00:54:20.076 --> 00:54:22.585
I was one minute off of BQ in Vermont,

1470
00:54:22.585 --> 00:54:24.631
and the next week I was running San Diego,

1471
00:54:24.631 --> 00:54:25.830
and I just happened to,

1472
00:54:25.830 --> 00:54:27.495
and I got the opportunity to run Vermont.

1473
00:54:27.495 --> 00:54:29.578
San Diego was really my goal race.

1474
00:54:29.578 --> 00:54:31.672
And I like looked at the elevation,

1475
00:54:31.672 --> 00:54:33.600
and I'm like, oh, it's gonna be fine.

1476
00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:36.213
Mile 21, I just like,

1477
00:54:36.213 --> 00:54:39.570
oh, and like six, eight weeks ago I was diagnosed

1478
00:54:39.570 --> 00:54:40.490
with type one diabetes.

1479
00:54:40.490 --> 00:54:41.323
Yeah, I know.

1480
00:54:41.323 --> 00:54:42.156
We're gonna get into that.

1481
00:54:42.156 --> 00:54:43.410
But anyways, but I had a crash,

1482
00:54:43.410 --> 00:54:45.310
but like the crashes with type one are

1483
00:54:45.310 --> 00:54:48.670
like a crash times a billion.

1484
00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:50.082
Like, it's just like you,

1485
00:54:50.082 --> 00:54:52.534
I was gonna start like just clawing up the highway.

1486
00:54:52.534 --> 00:54:54.030
I mean, it was bad.

1487
00:54:54.030 --> 00:54:56.075
So, this of course is a mile ascent

1488
00:54:56.075 --> 00:54:59.213
up this like fricking freeway in San Diego.

1489
00:54:59.213 --> 00:55:02.619
And I just, and that was like my second,

1490
00:55:02.619 --> 00:55:06.880
it brought me right back to like that moment in Utah

1491
00:55:06.880 --> 00:55:08.980
where I was like, I can't do this anymore.

1492
00:55:10.650 --> 00:55:13.300
And then I had to just be like, deal with it, dude.

1493
00:55:13.300 --> 00:55:15.370
You're not gonna BQ, you're not gonna get your best race.

1494
00:55:15.370 --> 00:55:16.680
Just finish the finish line

1495
00:55:16.680 --> 00:55:20.300
for the satisfaction of finishing what you started.

1496
00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:25.300
And yeah, it was kind of amazing to, in the,

1497
00:55:25.870 --> 00:55:28.510
I liked the process of being able

1498
00:55:28.510 --> 00:55:31.890
to recognize the disappointment, deal with it,

1499
00:55:31.890 --> 00:55:34.440
get rid of it, and just keep it going.

1500
00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:36.215
And then, you know, a little Gatorade helped too.

1501
00:55:36.215 --> 00:55:37.798
(Robin laughs)

1502
00:55:37.798 --> 00:55:39.130
That would help.

1503
00:55:39.130 --> 00:55:41.160
I mean, do you have these mornings where you're just,

1504
00:55:41.160 --> 00:55:43.010
you wake up and you're like, can't do it today.

1505
00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:44.684
Like, I'm just beat, I'm tired.

1506
00:55:44.684 --> 00:55:47.017
Oh my God, I'm tired so much.

1507
00:55:47.017 --> 00:55:49.143
People think, oh, you're doing all this stuff,

1508
00:55:49.143 --> 00:55:50.730
like you must just have the energy.

1509
00:55:50.730 --> 00:55:51.730
I mean, I don't drink coffee.

1510
00:55:51.730 --> 00:55:52.790
I'm not a caffeine person.

1511
00:55:52.790 --> 00:55:56.250
I just, I try to, you know, put in, you know,

1512
00:55:56.250 --> 00:55:57.877
what I think is gonna fuel my body the best

1513
00:55:57.877 --> 00:55:59.450
and that's not processed stuff.

1514
00:55:59.450 --> 00:56:02.426
But yes, I absolutely have those mornings

1515
00:56:02.426 --> 00:56:04.707
where I'm like, oh, I don't have it in me.

1516
00:56:04.707 --> 00:56:07.580
But then I think about how I'm gonna feel on the other end.

1517
00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:09.450
And that's always so nourished.

1518
00:56:09.450 --> 00:56:11.833
Like, before, so I'll wake up to teach my 6:00 AM classes

1519
00:56:11.833 --> 00:56:13.419
and I'm like, not today, man.

1520
00:56:13.419 --> 00:56:16.835
And then I leave those classes and it's like one rider

1521
00:56:16.835 --> 00:56:21.835
or one moment in the ride when everything comes together,

1522
00:56:22.850 --> 00:56:26.310
and I know that I'm putting out something into the world

1523
00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:27.970
that like is really authentic

1524
00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:31.790
and hopefully meaningful to someone.

1525
00:56:31.790 --> 00:56:34.430
So thinking it through to its conclusion, sort of.

1526
00:56:34.430 --> 00:56:36.641
Yeah, I always, even before a workout,

1527
00:56:36.641 --> 00:56:39.440
like I know before, like a really tough workout,

1528
00:56:39.440 --> 00:56:41.820
really tough run, or something like cross training,

1529
00:56:41.820 --> 00:56:43.278
I'm like, I don't wanna do this,

1530
00:56:43.278 --> 00:56:45.350
but I know how gratifying it's gonna feel.

1531
00:56:45.350 --> 00:56:48.035
Even if the workout wasn't what I expected,

1532
00:56:48.035 --> 00:56:49.550
it's always worth it.

1533
00:56:49.550 --> 00:56:50.719
Yeah, well, I think it's important

1534
00:56:50.719 --> 00:56:53.840
to communicate that because I think it would be easy

1535
00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:55.780
for somebody listening to this going,

1536
00:56:55.780 --> 00:56:56.900
well, she's just full of energy.

1537
00:56:56.900 --> 00:56:57.733
Like, that's not me.

1538
00:56:57.733 --> 00:56:58.566
I can't do that.

1539
00:56:58.566 --> 00:57:00.310
She's a freak of nature.

1540
00:57:00.310 --> 00:57:02.213
I mean, doing all this kind of stuff,

1541
00:57:02.213 --> 00:57:03.920
but, you know, this is not,

1542
00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:06.210
it's not like you've been doing this your whole life either.

1543
00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:07.439
Not even!

1544
00:57:07.439 --> 00:57:08.950
Like, not even a few years.

1545
00:57:08.950 --> 00:57:10.150
Like, this is just like-

1546
00:57:11.025 --> 00:57:13.670
