1818
01:07:47.389 --> 01:07:50.144
like, oh, well my instinct says do this,

1819
01:07:50.144 --> 01:07:51.750
but that's a terrible instinct.

1820
01:07:51.750 --> 01:07:52.810
Right!

1821
01:07:52.810 --> 01:07:54.190
That's a bad move, right.

1822
01:07:54.190 --> 01:07:55.581
So, like the gut check is good

1823
01:07:55.581 --> 01:07:59.335
if you're like doing the work, the inside work.

1824
01:07:59.335 --> 01:08:00.790
Yeah, I agree.

1825
01:08:00.790 --> 01:08:02.037
But you gotta get to that point.

1826
01:08:02.037 --> 01:08:04.010
But I do think that, you know,

1827
01:08:04.010 --> 01:08:07.420
barring sort of the big, the big mistake gut check,

1828
01:08:07.420 --> 01:08:12.036
you know, gut feelings, people are scared of failing.

1829
01:08:12.036 --> 01:08:14.644
And I think people need to get a little bit more comfortable

1830
01:08:14.644 --> 01:08:16.563
with failure.

1831
01:08:19.460 --> 01:08:23.943
I think if something feels scary to me,

1832
01:08:25.530 --> 01:08:29.560
and I can rationalize reasons

1833
01:08:29.560 --> 01:08:34.290
why it's not particularly harmful, I usually do it

1834
01:08:34.290 --> 01:08:39.290
because I think that fear can be a really good form of fuel

1835
01:08:40.670 --> 01:08:41.713
to do stuff.

1836
01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:45.070
And of course that's all that's balanced with like,

1837
01:08:45.070 --> 01:08:47.470
you know, using rational thoughts

1838
01:08:47.470 --> 01:08:50.040
to not put yourself in danger, but-

1839
01:08:50.040 --> 01:08:52.150
Well, I think it's okay to be afraid of failing,

1840
01:08:52.150 --> 01:08:54.648
but doing it anyway, you know what I mean?

1841
01:08:54.648 --> 01:08:56.547
It's sort of like acting as if in the face of that

1842
01:08:56.547 --> 01:08:58.010
and being willing to fail,

1843
01:08:58.010 --> 01:09:00.810
like embracing, you know, that failure is part of growth.

1844
01:09:00.810 --> 01:09:04.150
Like, if you're not like failing all the time at whatever,

1845
01:09:04.150 --> 01:09:06.860
then you're not pushing yourself hard enough to, you know,

1846
01:09:06.860 --> 01:09:08.010
to expand.

1847
01:09:08.010 --> 01:09:10.631
Yeah, I mean, I think that the complacency

1848
01:09:10.631 --> 01:09:14.879
and the feeling, if you're feeling comfortable all the time,

1849
01:09:14.879 --> 01:09:17.478
you probably need to be doing some more exciting shit.

1850
01:09:17.478 --> 01:09:18.311
Right.

1851
01:09:18.311 --> 01:09:19.205
Well, I think people that feel comfortable all the time

1852
01:09:19.205 --> 01:09:21.038
probably are not very happy.

1853
01:09:21.038 --> 01:09:23.220
Yeah, I mean, it goes back to like that 50%.

1854
01:09:23.220 --> 01:09:24.297
Like, you're just, okay.

1855
01:09:24.297 --> 01:09:26.770
You know, it's not necessarily like a clinical depression

1856
01:09:26.770 --> 01:09:30.119
or there's no, there's no real low.

1857
01:09:30.119 --> 01:09:32.391
But if you're like in that just mediocre state,

1858
01:09:32.391 --> 01:09:35.040
like, release it, release that mediocrity,

1859
01:09:35.040 --> 01:09:40.040
like there's so much out there that can really nourish you.

1860
01:09:41.150 --> 01:09:43.090
And then you can give that forward to the world.

1861
01:09:43.090 --> 01:09:44.756
Like, I don't know. I feel so much about like energy

1862
01:09:44.756 --> 01:09:48.520
and like giving rawness and authenticity out

1863
01:09:48.520 --> 01:09:52.273
in whatever form that means for you that's productive.

1864
01:09:53.470 --> 01:09:58.470
And I think people have a tendency

1865
01:09:58.470 --> 01:10:00.780
to just go about their days

1866
01:10:00.780 --> 01:10:03.670
and clocking like, oh, it's Monday or, oh, it's Tuesday.

1867
01:10:03.670 --> 01:10:04.730
Like, no.

1868
01:10:04.730 --> 01:10:08.347
Like, dude, the party doesn't have to continue

1869
01:10:08.347 --> 01:10:09.970
for you to keep dancing.

1870
01:10:09.970 --> 01:10:12.370
Like, just keep doing your damn thing.

1871
01:10:12.370 --> 01:10:14.033
And, I understand that people who are like,

1872
01:10:14.033 --> 01:10:15.920
oh, but I don't know what that is.

1873
01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:19.020
So, then like keep going back to point A and point B

1874
01:10:19.020 --> 01:10:19.980
and hitting refresh.

1875
01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:22.133
Like, it's not like, you know, I figured out,

1876
01:10:22.133 --> 01:10:24.522
oh, I like this, so that's just gonna be my thing forever.

1877
01:10:24.522 --> 01:10:26.430
Like, it's an iterative process.

1878
01:10:26.430 --> 01:10:29.015
Like, I might decide next week that I need to reset.

1879
01:10:29.015 --> 01:10:31.523
And like, and you just keep doing it.

1880
01:10:31.523 --> 01:10:34.096
And it's like that circuitous process

1881
01:10:34.096 --> 01:10:36.949
to quote unquote "success" or quote unquote "happiness."

1882
01:10:36.949 --> 01:10:41.320
It's showing up to that thing every day

1883
01:10:41.320 --> 01:10:44.200
and continuing that process, you know?

1884
01:10:44.200 --> 01:10:45.033
Yeah, I mean,

1885
01:10:45.033 --> 01:10:48.033
success is not a destination oriented thing.

1886
01:10:48.033 --> 01:10:49.970
It's how are you living and breathing

1887
01:10:49.970 --> 01:10:52.700
in each moment of your day, you know,

1888
01:10:52.700 --> 01:10:54.505
and are you embracing the journey that you're on,

1889
01:10:54.505 --> 01:10:57.050
and are you kind of waking up, you know,

1890
01:10:57.050 --> 01:10:58.940
excited for what the day might bring you?

1891
01:10:58.940 --> 01:11:00.270
I mean, that's success.

1892
01:11:00.270 --> 01:11:01.439
And, you know, listen,

1893
01:11:01.439 --> 01:11:05.130
I'm sure we both know wildly successful, you know,

1894
01:11:05.130 --> 01:11:06.922
rich people that are really unhappy

1895
01:11:06.922 --> 01:11:09.543
who have pursued happiness in the form

1896
01:11:09.543 --> 01:11:13.040
that, you know, was portrayed to us as the path to,

1897
01:11:13.040 --> 01:11:15.482
you know, all of that and then getting there and going,

1898
01:11:15.482 --> 01:11:17.450
you know, what the fuck?

1899
01:11:17.450 --> 01:11:18.659
This is not what I thought it would be.

1900
01:11:18.659 --> 01:11:22.100
And that creates like this crazy existential crisis

1901
01:11:22.100 --> 01:11:22.933
where you're like, well, what now?

1902
01:11:22.933 --> 01:11:25.920
You know, like everything I did was to get to this thing

1903
01:11:25.920 --> 01:11:29.677
and now, well maybe if I just, if I get the next car

1904
01:11:29.677 --> 01:11:31.950
or whatever it is that will solve it,

1905
01:11:31.950 --> 01:11:33.808
and then doing that enough to understand like,

1906
01:11:33.808 --> 01:11:35.770
oh, that's never gonna work.

1907
01:11:35.770 --> 01:11:36.849
Now what?

1908
01:11:36.849 --> 01:11:38.182
Yeah, and I know that

1909
01:11:38.182 --> 01:11:43.089
that's a common sort of like American story

1910
01:11:43.089 --> 01:11:45.170
or Western story.

1911
01:11:45.170 --> 01:11:47.540
And I just totally can't relate to that anymore.

1912
01:11:47.540 --> 01:11:50.670
Like, sometimes it's hard for me to advise on that

1913
01:11:50.670 --> 01:11:52.263
or breaking out of that.

1914
01:11:53.130 --> 01:11:55.910
But you must have seen partners in your law firm.

1915
01:11:55.910 --> 01:11:56.743
Oh yeah, no.

1916
01:11:56.743 --> 01:11:58.366
Who are wildly overpaid or not overpaid,

1917
01:11:58.366 --> 01:12:02.667
but just incredibly well paid, working incredible hours.

1918
01:12:02.667 --> 01:12:04.630
And it's not like when you make partner,

1919
01:12:04.630 --> 01:12:06.562
like, oh, they give you the key to the executive washroom

1920
01:12:06.562 --> 01:12:07.700
and it's all good.

1921
01:12:07.700 --> 01:12:10.800
Like, it's just more work and more responsibility.

1922
01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:12.660
And then, and a lot of, you know,

1923
01:12:12.660 --> 01:12:16.165
I don't regret my life as a corporate lawyer either.

1924
01:12:16.165 --> 01:12:17.440
Like, I learned a lot.

1925
01:12:17.440 --> 01:12:19.320
I met amazing brilliant people,

1926
01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:21.420
but it was definitely not the life for me.

1927
01:12:21.420 --> 01:12:23.950
And I saw a lot of unhappy people who, you know,

1928
01:12:23.950 --> 01:12:26.460
medicated themselves through buying things,

1929
01:12:26.460 --> 01:12:29.250
always kind of living just a little bit

1930
01:12:29.250 --> 01:12:30.451
beyond what they could afford.

1931
01:12:30.451 --> 01:12:32.544
So, you're perpetually enslaved

1932
01:12:32.544 --> 01:12:35.406
and you're really never able to part with it,

1933
01:12:35.406 --> 01:12:37.290
like you're shackled to it.

1934
01:12:37.290 --> 01:12:38.123
Oh yeah.

1935
01:12:38.123 --> 01:12:40.850
And I remember, I mean, I was gonna,

1936
01:12:40.850 --> 01:12:42.770
it was like what the young associates were doing.

1937
01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:44.072
People were buying a property,

1938
01:12:44.072 --> 01:12:46.250
and I was like, all right, I should buy an apartment, right?

1939
01:12:46.250 --> 01:12:47.240
That's what makes sense.

1940
01:12:47.240 --> 01:12:48.740
Like, I should move in with this guy,

1941
01:12:48.740 --> 01:12:50.450
and we should buy an apartment,

1942
01:12:50.450 --> 01:12:52.350
and I should have a kid, right?

1943
01:12:52.350 --> 01:12:54.340
And then it's like, no joke,

1944
01:12:54.340 --> 01:12:57.358
it was the night before I was supposed to sign the contract

1945
01:12:57.358 --> 01:13:01.340
on this apartment, I was like, but it's a great deal,

1946
01:13:01.340 --> 01:13:02.360
and it's in Williamsburg,

1947
01:13:02.360 --> 01:13:03.820
and it's up and coming and blah, blah, blah.

1948
01:13:03.820 --> 01:13:04.679
I was like, what are you doing?

1949
01:13:04.679 --> 01:13:06.680
You're gonna be shackled with a mortgage,

1950
01:13:06.680 --> 01:13:08.610
and you don't even want this job.

1951
01:13:08.610 --> 01:13:11.210
And that was two years before I actually left.

1952
01:13:11.210 --> 01:13:13.090
And it took two years to build the courage.

1953
01:13:13.090 --> 01:13:14.930
So, I don't say like, flippantly,

1954
01:13:14.930 --> 01:13:17.580
like, ah, just like quit your job and like do epic things.

1955
01:13:17.580 --> 01:13:19.003
Like, I get it.

1956
01:13:20.021 --> 01:13:20.979
These are hard things.

1957
01:13:20.979 --> 01:13:22.570
I was definitely at that crossroads.

1958
01:13:22.570 --> 01:13:24.020
And I even had that, you know,

1959
01:13:24.020 --> 01:13:25.680
that thought process that it was like,

1960
01:13:25.680 --> 01:13:26.620
I'm gonna buy the apartment

1961
01:13:26.620 --> 01:13:29.068
and then I'm gonna feel like empowered by that.

1962
01:13:29.068 --> 01:13:33.158
And really like it so had nothing to do with buying anything

1963
01:13:33.158 --> 01:13:34.520
or owning anything.

1964
01:13:34.520 --> 01:13:36.074
And it was all like truly pushing

1965
01:13:36.074 --> 01:13:37.613
and pulling my own body weight.

1966
01:13:37.613 --> 01:13:40.180
It was the most basic thing I could figure out.

1967
01:13:40.180 --> 01:13:42.530
So even though that was two years prior,

1968
01:13:42.530 --> 01:13:45.427
there's this seed, little seedling that's growing,

1969
01:13:45.427 --> 01:13:47.500
that's going, yeah, don't get too invested

1970
01:13:47.500 --> 01:13:48.990
in what's going on here.

1971
01:13:48.990 --> 01:13:49.823
Yeah, and it was like,

1972
01:13:49.823 --> 01:13:51.730
and then it was like live below your means,

1973
01:13:51.730 --> 01:13:53.000
like save up money.

1974
01:13:53.000 --> 01:13:53.990
And if I hadn't done that,

1975
01:13:53.990 --> 01:13:57.748
there is no possible way I could have left.

1976
01:13:57.748 --> 01:13:58.640
There's no way.

1977
01:13:58.640 --> 01:13:59.819
'Cause I wouldn't have been able to afford to leave

1978
01:13:59.819 --> 01:14:02.103
and pay law school loans and, you know, life.

1979
01:14:02.103 --> 01:14:03.826
Yeah, that's the one thing I always say

1980
01:14:03.826 --> 01:14:05.909
to young people is like live lean.

1981
01:14:05.909 --> 01:14:06.867
Like, don't buy stuff.

1982
01:14:06.867 --> 01:14:11.867
Like, the best insurance policy on your freedom

1983
01:14:11.870 --> 01:14:15.740
and being able to make choices is to live lean.

1984
01:14:15.740 --> 01:14:18.640
Live as lean as possible when you're young.

1985
01:14:18.640 --> 01:14:19.680
Totally agreed.

1986
01:14:19.680 --> 01:14:21.410
Totally. And I'm so grateful that I,

1987
01:14:21.410 --> 01:14:23.737
I actually never, I rarely think about that.

1988
01:14:23.737 --> 01:14:25.680
And that was a real crossroads for me

1989
01:14:25.680 --> 01:14:27.200
that I didn't even recognize as one.

1990
01:14:27.200 --> 01:14:29.320
I was just like, ah, I'll figure this out later.

1991
01:14:29.320 --> 01:14:31.840
And then a few years later I'm like, oh my God,

1992
01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:32.790
I don't have a mortgage.

1993
01:14:32.790 --> 01:14:34.300
Like, this is major.

1994
01:14:34.300 --> 01:14:36.230
I can pick up and leave whenever I want.

1995
01:14:36.230 --> 01:14:40.110
I mean, freedom, portability, like, you know,

1996
01:14:40.110 --> 01:14:43.520
it's a very, those are very real concepts in my life.

1997
01:14:43.520 --> 01:14:45.870
All right, so type one diabetes.

1998
01:14:45.870 --> 01:14:46.703
Type one, man.

1999
01:14:46.703 --> 01:14:49.730
Like, that was so shocking when you just posted it

2000
01:14:49.730 --> 01:14:51.292
on Instagram one day.

2001
01:14:51.292 --> 01:14:52.790
And I was like, what?

2002
01:14:52.790 --> 01:14:53.623
I know.

2003
01:14:53.623 --> 01:14:55.374
Like, out of the blue. Like, first of all,

2004
01:14:55.374 --> 01:14:58.016
I didn't even know that that's the way it could work.

2005
01:14:58.016 --> 01:15:00.539
Like, I thought like type one was something,

2006
01:15:00.539 --> 01:15:02.990
of course type two we know there's a lot

2007
01:15:02.990 --> 01:15:04.390
of lifestyle components to that.

2008
01:15:04.390 --> 01:15:05.580
Type one is very different.

2009
01:15:05.580 --> 01:15:07.828
And that's something that, you know,

2010
01:15:07.828 --> 01:15:10.300
I thought was sort of more of like a genetic thing

2011
01:15:10.300 --> 01:15:13.129
that would've been diagnosed like very much earlier in life.

2012
01:15:13.129 --> 01:15:13.962
So yeah,

2013
01:15:13.962 --> 01:15:16.510
type one used to be called juvenile diabetes.

2014
01:15:16.510 --> 01:15:18.730
And it was just, I mean,

2015
01:15:18.730 --> 01:15:21.590
I just came back from a month long trip in India,

2016
01:15:21.590 --> 01:15:23.360
and I thought I was jet lagged or something,

2017
01:15:23.360 --> 01:15:25.383
and I felt really dehydrated.

2018
01:15:25.383 --> 01:15:27.923
And my mom's a physician and she was like,

2019
01:15:27.923 --> 01:15:29.950
this doesn't sound right. We need to get blood work.

2020
01:15:29.950 --> 01:15:31.564
So, it was like Tuesday, blood work,

2021
01:15:31.564 --> 01:15:35.563
Wednesday, endocrinologist, Thursday, I'm on insulin, like.

2022
01:15:36.450 --> 01:15:37.283
Out of the blue.

2023
01:15:37.283 --> 01:15:38.551
And they did an A1C test,

2024
01:15:38.551 --> 01:15:40.528
which basically goes back in your blood work

2025
01:15:40.528 --> 01:15:43.570
and like measures your glucose levels and all this stuff.

2026
01:15:43.570 --> 01:15:44.740
And for months, I mean,

2027
01:15:44.740 --> 01:15:47.810
I'd done three marathons with type one and I had no idea.

2028
01:15:47.810 --> 01:15:50.240
Like, I was just running at such a high level

2029
01:15:50.240 --> 01:15:52.951
that I was burning just enough sugar to not be hospitalized.

2030
01:15:52.951 --> 01:15:54.284
But essentially type one means

2031
01:15:54.284 --> 01:15:57.458
that your pancreas makes little or no insulin.

2032
01:15:57.458 --> 01:15:59.119
So, it's just an autoimmune disease.

2033
01:15:59.119 --> 01:16:03.189
And my pancreas just like, it's on strike forever.

2034
01:16:03.189 --> 01:16:04.022
Wow.

2035
01:16:04.022 --> 01:16:05.057
Like, it's just done.

2036
01:16:05.057 --> 01:16:06.418
And you were walking around

2037
01:16:06.418 --> 01:16:08.570
and running around with that for-

2038
01:16:08.570 --> 01:16:09.403
For months.

2039
01:16:09.403 --> 01:16:10.846
For like at least four months.

2040
01:16:10.846 --> 01:16:13.985
I mean, you could have just had a serious problem.

2041
01:16:13.985 --> 01:16:17.900
A big, actually most type ones are diagnosed

2042
01:16:17.900 --> 01:16:20.330
in the hospital 'cause there aren't any symptoms really.

2043
01:16:20.330 --> 01:16:23.750
And they have such high sugars that, I mean, your organs,

2044
01:16:23.750 --> 01:16:25.010
I mean, you have ketoacidosis

2045
01:16:25.010 --> 01:16:27.410
and tons of terrible stuff happens.

2046
01:16:27.410 --> 01:16:29.512
And the effects of running with

2047
01:16:29.512 --> 01:16:33.360
that high of sugar is pretty major for every single organ

2048
01:16:33.360 --> 01:16:34.340
in your body.

2049
01:16:34.340 --> 01:16:37.240
So, it's unbelievable.

2050
01:16:37.240 --> 01:16:38.620
And of course people are shocked.

2051
01:16:38.620 --> 01:16:41.440
I mean, it's like, I'm a really healthy person otherwise.

2052
01:16:41.440 --> 01:16:43.290
Very healthy.

2053
01:16:43.290 --> 01:16:46.700
And when my endo, I was like,

2054
01:16:46.700 --> 01:16:48.900
oh my God, is this pancreatic cancer, something crazy?

2055
01:16:48.900 --> 01:16:50.750
I mean, how could this happen to me?

2056
01:16:50.750 --> 01:16:54.020
And it was like, again, one of those pivotal moments

2057
01:16:54.020 --> 01:16:55.020
where the endocrinologist is like,

2058
01:16:55.020 --> 01:16:56.350
you have type one diabetes.

2059
01:16:56.350 --> 01:16:59.470
And I was like, no way.

2060
01:16:59.470 --> 01:17:00.990
And then I was like, what do I do about it?

2061
01:17:00.990 --> 01:17:02.120
How do I run ultras?

2062
01:17:02.120 --> 01:17:03.750
Like, literally that was like the first thing

2063
01:17:03.750 --> 01:17:04.920
that came out of my mouth.

2064
01:17:04.920 --> 01:17:07.130
And she was like pulling out like, you know,

2065
01:17:07.130 --> 01:17:08.479
this was like an emergency visit.

2066
01:17:08.479 --> 01:17:10.290
I didn't have an endocrinologist before.

2067
01:17:10.290 --> 01:17:11.880
So, she really didn't know me.

2068
01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:13.240
She just was looking at this blood work

2069
01:17:13.240 --> 01:17:14.744
and like diagnosing me on paper.

2070
01:17:14.744 --> 01:17:16.550
She's like, that's your first question?

2071
01:17:16.550 --> 01:17:19.300
Yeah, well she pulls out this like, you know,

2072
01:17:20.320 --> 01:17:23.590
USDA like food portion map or whatever.

2073
01:17:23.590 --> 01:17:26.685
I was like, listen, chick,

2074
01:17:26.685 --> 01:17:28.870
that's not the conversation we're having.

2075
01:17:28.870 --> 01:17:31.370
We're talking about how I can run a 100 mile race.

2076
01:17:32.240 --> 01:17:34.840
And my friend Steven England is an incredible athlete.

2077
01:17:34.840 --> 01:17:36.180
He's done like Leadville,

2078
01:17:36.180 --> 01:17:39.490
and he's training for a 200 mile race around Tahoe

2079
01:17:39.490 --> 01:17:40.323
in September.

2080
01:17:40.323 --> 01:17:41.260
And he's a type one diabetic.

2081
01:17:41.260 --> 01:17:43.100
He's had type one since he was 14.

2082
01:17:43.100 --> 01:17:44.117
He was one of the first people I called.

2083
01:17:44.117 --> 01:17:46.900
It was like my mom, Steven.

2084
01:17:46.900 --> 01:17:49.580
'Cause I just needed like that...

2085
01:17:49.580 --> 01:17:50.820
Thankfully I know him

2086
01:17:50.820 --> 01:17:52.195
'cause so much of the information out there,

2087
01:17:52.195 --> 01:17:54.780
even for athletes with type one,

2088
01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:55.696
there's a handful of Olympians,

2089
01:17:55.696 --> 01:17:57.810
but there's, it's not really,

2090
01:17:57.810 --> 01:17:59.920
it's not really out there in terms of like-

2091
01:17:59.920 --> 01:18:01.380
There's a professional cycling team

2092
01:18:01.380 --> 01:18:02.336
called Team Type One.

2093
01:18:02.336 --> 01:18:03.169
My friend Steven's on it.

2094
01:18:03.169 --> 01:18:04.002
Okay, cool.

2095
01:18:04.002 --> 01:18:06.410
Novo Nordisk is the team.

2096
01:18:06.410 --> 01:18:07.243
The sponsor, right?

2097
01:18:07.243 --> 01:18:08.076
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

2098
01:18:08.076 --> 01:18:09.845
So, my friend Steven introduced those guys,

2099
01:18:09.845 --> 01:18:12.270
and he's part of the running component of that.

2100
01:18:12.270 --> 01:18:13.103
Oh cool.

2101
01:18:13.103 --> 01:18:14.460
So, there's a running team part of that.

2102
01:18:14.460 --> 01:18:16.800
Yeah, so it started as a cycling team,

2103
01:18:16.800 --> 01:18:18.090
and they've done like incredible stuff.

2104
01:18:18.090 --> 01:18:20.480
Yeah, there are like amazing cyclists on the team.

2105
01:18:20.480 --> 01:18:21.630
Amazing, amazing.

2106
01:18:21.630 --> 01:18:24.610
I mean, they're truly professional athletes.

2107
01:18:24.610 --> 01:18:25.944
And then there's a tri team

2108
01:18:25.944 --> 01:18:27.920
and then there's the running team.

2109
01:18:27.920 --> 01:18:31.000
And a lot of the runners are marathoners

2110
01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:32.880
and ultra marathoners.

2111
01:18:32.880 --> 01:18:33.842
So, Steven was like,

2112
01:18:33.842 --> 01:18:37.190
you're gonna have to figure out your own like treatment,

2113
01:18:37.190 --> 01:18:40.028
but yes, like this, this, and that are possible.

2114
01:18:40.028 --> 01:18:40.986
So, I felt better.

2115
01:18:40.986 --> 01:18:43.440
Right, so how does it, so like what does it mean?

2116
01:18:43.440 --> 01:18:44.824
Like, when you go out to train, like how do you-

2117
01:18:44.824 --> 01:18:48.212
So, I'm constantly battling low blood sugar.

2118
01:18:48.212 --> 01:18:49.907
That's the biggest thing for me.

2119
01:18:49.907 --> 01:18:51.290
You okay right now?

2120
01:18:51.290 --> 01:18:52.370
Yes, I'm okay.

2121
01:18:52.370 --> 01:18:54.970
I can, I mean, I can sense it thankfully.

2122
01:18:54.970 --> 01:18:57.175
I mean, because I have body awareness.

2123
01:18:57.175 --> 01:18:58.061
How do you know?

2124
01:18:58.061 --> 01:18:59.394
Do you get lightheaded or how does it come on?

2125
01:18:59.394 --> 01:19:01.227
It's like...

2126
01:19:02.150 --> 01:19:05.863
It's a combination of feeling voraciously hungry

2127
01:19:08.660 --> 01:19:11.506
and brain fog.

2128
01:19:11.506 --> 01:19:13.300
Like, for me, that's how I know.

2129
01:19:13.300 --> 01:19:14.430
It's like, I just feel fuzzy.

2130
01:19:14.430 --> 01:19:16.750
Like, your brain needs sugar to function, right.

2131
01:19:16.750 --> 01:19:19.163
If you go below a certain level, you'll be in a coma.

2132
01:19:19.163 --> 01:19:24.163
And most people their pancreas produces insulin

2133
01:19:26.220 --> 01:19:28.160
to sort of regulate that automatically.

2134
01:19:28.160 --> 01:19:29.421
As long as you're eating, you know,

2135
01:19:29.421 --> 01:19:30.380
in proportion to your body weight

2136
01:19:30.380 --> 01:19:32.470
and you're not, you know, being excess with your lifestyle,

2137
01:19:32.470 --> 01:19:34.480
like most people are pretty good.

2138
01:19:34.480 --> 01:19:38.030
And for me, I have an insulin pump

2139
01:19:38.030 --> 01:19:41.620
that gives me an eye drop of insulin every seven minutes.

2140
01:19:41.620 --> 01:19:43.100
Thankfully I have this pod

2141
01:19:43.100 --> 01:19:48.100
because the pod combined with the glucose monitoring device

2142
01:19:49.380 --> 01:19:52.336
that I have, both, I mean, technology is everything.

2143
01:19:52.336 --> 01:19:54.390
Like, I don't know how I would do it

2144
01:19:54.390 --> 01:19:56.880
if I had to carry needles around on like a four hour run

2145
01:19:56.880 --> 01:19:58.900
or something, like it would be insane.

2146
01:19:58.900 --> 01:20:02.855
So, basically I have a little personal monitoring device

2147
01:20:02.855 --> 01:20:06.214
that I adjust my insulin based on like what I do.

2148
01:20:06.214 --> 01:20:07.540
And it's like, so hold on.

2149
01:20:07.540 --> 01:20:08.854
So, it's like implanted in you?

2150
01:20:08.854 --> 01:20:10.687
Yeah, it's right here.

2151
01:20:10.687 --> 01:20:11.645
Oh, wow.

2152
01:20:11.645 --> 01:20:13.978
It's literally under my skin, but I move it.

2153
01:20:13.978 --> 01:20:15.827
So, I change this every three days,

2154
01:20:15.827 --> 01:20:17.070
and sometimes I wear it on my arm.

2155
01:20:17.070 --> 01:20:18.493
And I remember when, you know,

2156
01:20:18.493 --> 01:20:19.907
I was going through the training,

2157
01:20:19.907 --> 01:20:21.930
the lady's like, you know, you can put it on your arm,

2158
01:20:21.930 --> 01:20:23.910
you have to move it or else it creates scar tissue,

2159
01:20:23.910 --> 01:20:25.791
'cause it's really a needle that goes under your skin.

2160
01:20:25.791 --> 01:20:28.400
And I was like, oh, I would never wear it on my arm.

2161
01:20:28.400 --> 01:20:30.410
God, I like would never want to be public.

2162
01:20:30.410 --> 01:20:31.560
And then of course like 10 days later,

2163
01:20:31.560 --> 01:20:34.030
I'm like, what's up Instagram, here's my pod.

2164
01:20:34.030 --> 01:20:35.223
I'm loud and I'm proud.

2165
01:20:36.300 --> 01:20:38.683
But like I had to own it. Like, I just had to own it.

2166
01:20:38.683 --> 01:20:41.604
It's like that's my reality.

2167
01:20:41.604 --> 01:20:45.910
And they had never seen somebody

2168
01:20:45.910 --> 01:20:48.540
so aggressively pursue like all this technology.

2169
01:20:48.540 --> 01:20:50.605
I was like, give it all to me! I want all the data.

2170
01:20:50.605 --> 01:20:52.440
I want all the information.

2171
01:20:52.440 --> 01:20:53.880
I wanna be empowered.

2172
01:20:53.880 --> 01:20:58.180
And I mean, seriously, there wasn't even,

2173
01:20:58.180 --> 01:21:00.811
I didn't even waste an hour being like why?

2174
01:21:00.811 --> 01:21:03.119
I was like, you're just gonna have to deal with this

2175
01:21:03.119 --> 01:21:06.400
and you're strong enough, so go.

2176
01:21:06.400 --> 01:21:07.450
And that's what happened.

2177
01:21:07.450 --> 01:21:09.303
So, it sucks. I mean, I'm not gonna lie to you.

2178
01:21:09.303 --> 01:21:12.217
It's a 24/7 tight rope walk of making sure I'm

2179
01:21:12.217 --> 01:21:15.130
in between high and low.

2180
01:21:15.130 --> 01:21:17.000
And as an athlete, it's more battling lows

2181
01:21:17.000 --> 01:21:18.320
than anything else.

2182
01:21:18.320 --> 01:21:20.797
And, you know, I'll teach three recycling classes in a row

2183
01:21:20.797 --> 01:21:24.885
and like, I'll just have to like have something there,

2184
01:21:24.885 --> 01:21:26.160
like always, you know?

2185
01:21:26.160 --> 01:21:27.890
Right, you just have to plan ahead a little bit,

2186
01:21:27.890 --> 01:21:29.332
and just be on top and monitoring it.

2187
01:21:29.332 --> 01:21:31.510
But the only time you ever really felt

2188
01:21:31.510 --> 01:21:32.955
like you were almost getting in trouble was running

2189
01:21:32.955 --> 01:21:34.370
that marathon?

2190
01:21:34.370 --> 01:21:35.203
In San Diego.

2191
01:21:35.203 --> 01:21:37.330
I mean, I was like about to,

2192
01:21:37.330 --> 01:21:39.337
like without like something, some sugar in my system,

2193
01:21:39.337 --> 01:21:41.693
and it, there was no rhyme or reason for it.

2194
01:21:41.693 --> 01:21:44.370
It's like, I feel just like my training runs.

2195
01:21:44.370 --> 01:21:47.750
I feel just like a week, literally a week prior,

2196
01:21:47.750 --> 01:21:50.800
same distance, same fuel, same goose,

2197
01:21:50.800 --> 01:21:53.150
same whatever, same game plan.

2198
01:21:53.150 --> 01:21:54.500
Body just reacted differently.

2199
01:21:54.500 --> 01:21:56.750
And it's just like, you know, that's the unpredictability.

2200
01:21:56.750 --> 01:21:58.850
80% of the time,

2201
01:21:58.850 --> 01:22:00.900
this is how Steven explained it to me and it's totally true.

2202
01:22:00.900 --> 01:22:02.360
80% of the time,

2203
01:22:02.360 --> 01:22:04.570
once you have an understanding of your diabetes,

2204
01:22:04.570 --> 01:22:06.220
like you got it under control,

2205
01:22:06.220 --> 01:22:08.940
but then there's that 20% that like your body

2206
01:22:08.940 --> 01:22:10.160
just goes crazy.

2207
01:22:10.160 --> 01:22:11.947
Like, you just don't know.

2208
01:22:11.947 --> 01:22:12.993
So you just have to always be ready

2209
01:22:12.993 --> 01:22:15.224
in the event of that unpredictable moment.

2210
01:22:15.224 --> 01:22:18.159
Yeah, I mean, it's like, snacks.

2211
01:22:18.159 --> 01:22:21.510
Like, I mean, I literally like at any given time of day,

2212
01:22:21.510 --> 01:22:25.177
I probably have like a honey stinger in my sports bra.

2213
01:22:25.177 --> 01:22:27.390
(Robin laughs)

2214
01:22:27.390 --> 01:22:29.340
Like, I probably have like food tucked away somewhere,

2215
01:22:29.340 --> 01:22:30.173
even though I'm wearing

2216
01:22:30.173 --> 01:22:31.649
like all of two articles of clothing.

2217
01:22:31.649 --> 01:22:34.260
(everyone laughs)

2218
01:22:34.260 --> 01:22:35.093
Cool.

2219
01:22:35.093 --> 01:22:36.398
So, I want to talk about kind of,

2220
01:22:36.398 --> 01:22:38.208
when you gave your talk at Catalyst,

2221
01:22:38.208 --> 01:22:41.463
it was really the theme of it was like tell your own story.

2222
01:22:41.463 --> 01:22:42.296
Yeah.

2223
01:22:42.296 --> 01:22:43.420
Like, be your own story.

2224
01:22:43.420 --> 01:22:45.600
And how does that...

2225
01:22:47.337 --> 01:22:51.460
You know, how do you kind of communicate that

2226
01:22:51.460 --> 01:22:53.070
to the people that you work with

2227
01:22:53.070 --> 01:22:55.520
to help raise their bar of their experience?

2228
01:22:55.520 --> 01:22:56.880
'Cause I mean, a big part of,

2229
01:22:56.880 --> 01:22:58.661
I think a big part of living a passionate life,

2230
01:22:58.661 --> 01:23:02.006
and you exude this, is giving back, right?

2231
01:23:02.006 --> 01:23:03.940
Like, a part of what gets you out of the bed

2232
01:23:03.940 --> 01:23:05.230
in the morning is that you know

2233
01:23:05.230 --> 01:23:06.610
that you're helping other people

2234
01:23:06.610 --> 01:23:08.920
and that you're having a positive impact on others,

2235
01:23:08.920 --> 01:23:10.342
and that's a huge motivating factor

2236
01:23:10.342 --> 01:23:12.040
and it's a big part of, you know,

2237
01:23:12.040 --> 01:23:13.670
basically your own personal fulfillment

2238
01:23:13.670 --> 01:23:15.590
like happiness equation, right?

2239
01:23:15.590 --> 01:23:18.680
So, what is the mechanism by which you are able,

2240
01:23:18.680 --> 01:23:21.063
like there's, let me back up.

2241
01:23:21.930 --> 01:23:26.930
There's nothing more powerful than a good story well told.

2242
01:23:27.056 --> 01:23:32.056
So, and the ability to effectively communicate a good story,

2243
01:23:32.970 --> 01:23:35.600
whether it's your story or somebody else's story,

2244
01:23:35.600 --> 01:23:38.016
is one of the most empowering and inspirational things

2245
01:23:38.016 --> 01:23:38.970
that you can do.

2246
01:23:38.970 --> 01:23:39.887
And you're very good at it.

2247
01:23:39.887 --> 01:23:43.490
And your lifestyle is like an extrapolation of that, right?

2248
01:23:43.490 --> 01:23:45.330
So, how does that like kind of,

2249
01:23:45.330 --> 01:23:47.090
how do you infuse that into the people

2250
01:23:47.090 --> 01:23:48.723
that you're interacting with?

2251
01:23:50.048 --> 01:23:51.470
That's a pretty vague question.

2252
01:23:51.470 --> 01:23:52.949
No, I mean, kind of.

2253
01:23:52.949 --> 01:23:53.907
(Robin laughs)

2254
01:23:53.907 --> 01:23:54.740
Do you know what I'm getting at though?

2255
01:23:54.740 --> 01:23:55.968
Well, I think you're asking-

2256
01:23:55.968 --> 01:23:56.930
Like, I really just wanna talk about story.

2257
01:23:56.930 --> 01:23:59.080
Yeah, well, how do you, how do you own it?

2258
01:23:59.080 --> 01:24:03.550
I mean, I think it really goes back to people talk

2259
01:24:05.090 --> 01:24:06.920
when they're figuring out like a startup or whatever.

2260
01:24:06.920 --> 01:24:08.320
It's like, what are your brand values?

2261
01:24:08.320 --> 01:24:10.500
Like, we have so much conversation

2262
01:24:10.500 --> 01:24:15.500
around like how marketing should look or feel or sound.

2263
01:24:17.270 --> 01:24:19.491
And what I really like to encourage people

2264
01:24:19.491 --> 01:24:22.199
to think about is,

2265
01:24:22.199 --> 01:24:25.380
you are the CEO of your body, like head to toe.

2266
01:24:25.380 --> 01:24:26.697
What are you putting out into the world

2267
01:24:26.697 --> 01:24:31.697
in what you do and what you say and how you feel?

2268
01:24:31.810 --> 01:24:34.590
How you feel is one of the best things

2269
01:24:34.590 --> 01:24:35.887
that you can do is take care of yourself.

2270
01:24:35.887 --> 01:24:37.750
That's a service to others.

2271
01:24:37.750 --> 01:24:38.900
That's not selfish.

2272
01:24:38.900 --> 01:24:42.223
That's taking care of your own home,

2273
01:24:42.223 --> 01:24:45.670
your own, like, you know, body

2274
01:24:45.670 --> 01:24:47.930
and being of service to the world

2275
01:24:47.930 --> 01:24:49.450
in whatever capacity you can be.

2276
01:24:49.450 --> 01:24:53.283
And I think the owning, the empowerment of it,

2277
01:24:54.150 --> 01:24:55.840
I discovered it through physical activity.

2278
01:24:55.840 --> 01:25:00.840
Like, I really think that some type of fitness is a way

2279
01:25:01.570 --> 01:25:06.570
to tap into unlocking your why.

2280
01:25:08.330 --> 01:25:11.400
And I think when you identify your why,

2281
01:25:11.400 --> 01:25:13.970
then all these other things fall into place.

2282
01:25:13.970 --> 01:25:15.540
You have a little bit more clarity,

2283
01:25:15.540 --> 01:25:17.632
like the cleaning, you know, the cleaning of the cob web

2284
01:25:17.632 --> 01:25:19.145
so to speak that you sort of mentioned.

2285
01:25:19.145 --> 01:25:23.793
Like, making sure your internal compass is, is dialed in.

2286
01:25:25.260 --> 01:25:29.620
I do think that, I really do think that sweat is cathartic

2287
01:25:29.620 --> 01:25:31.200
in a really major way.

2288
01:25:31.200 --> 01:25:33.700
And not to say that, you know, somebody's gonna go for a run

2289
01:25:33.700 --> 01:25:34.940
and decide like, oh, I wanna be an ultra runner.

2290
01:25:34.940 --> 01:25:37.770
Oh, I wanna, you know, be X, Y, Z in related to sports

2291
01:25:37.770 --> 01:25:39.820
or wellness, not to say that at all.

2292
01:25:39.820 --> 01:25:42.490
But I do think that there's some clarity that comes with it.

2293
01:25:42.490 --> 01:25:45.648
And I think from there, from sort of that base,

2294
01:25:45.648 --> 01:25:49.780
then you start to realize like the literal

2295
01:25:49.780 --> 01:25:53.190
and visual conversations that you're having with the world.

2296
01:25:53.190 --> 01:25:54.170
And I think,

2297
01:25:54.170 --> 01:25:58.290
especially because of the social media fragmentation,

2298
01:25:59.639 --> 01:26:01.260
we kind of are looking at things

2299
01:26:01.260 --> 01:26:06.260
in like this really jarring like Instagram post,

2300
01:26:07.620 --> 01:26:11.240
Facebook post, tweet, FOMO, YOLO, like all these,

2301
01:26:11.240 --> 01:26:13.210
like, I mean, really that's just like,

2302
01:26:13.210 --> 01:26:15.310
that's a modern day just existential

2303
01:26:15.310 --> 01:26:18.240
like question of nowness.

2304
01:26:19.407 --> 01:26:21.461
And I think when we're thinking

2305
01:26:21.461 --> 01:26:24.656
of what our legacy is gonna be

2306
01:26:24.656 --> 01:26:27.734
and all these really lofty questions,

2307
01:26:27.734 --> 01:26:32.734
at the end of the day, it's just like are you living

2308
01:26:33.089 --> 01:26:36.500
and writing stories that are gonna be really juicy

2309
01:26:36.500 --> 01:26:37.333
when you're 90?

2310
01:26:38.520 --> 01:26:40.988
Are you doing something that matters in your gut?

2311
01:26:40.988 --> 01:26:45.988
Not because it's, you know, some lofty linear story,

2312
01:26:46.850 --> 01:26:48.950
literally that, you know, that could be fictionalized,

2313
01:26:48.950 --> 01:26:52.500
but because you're making connections with people

2314
01:26:52.500 --> 01:26:55.584
and building community and like, you know, eating real food

2315
01:26:55.584 --> 01:26:57.310
and just like all these little things,

2316
01:26:57.310 --> 01:27:00.270
like it's all about the little things.

2317
01:27:00.270 --> 01:27:03.210
And what I think my point is when you're saying,

2318
01:27:03.210 --> 01:27:04.640
write your story, when I'm saying,

2319
01:27:04.640 --> 01:27:06.570
write your story and own the pen,

2320
01:27:06.570 --> 01:27:09.259
it's more like, are the little decisions and little thoughts

2321
01:27:09.259 --> 01:27:11.617
that you're having every day adding up

2322
01:27:11.617 --> 01:27:14.900
to this larger picture that you see for yourself?

2323
01:27:14.900 --> 01:27:18.513
And I think people should take excitement in that.

2324
01:27:18.513 --> 01:27:21.129
Like, there is just so much power in that ability

2325
01:27:21.129 --> 01:27:24.260
to literally stop the thing that you're doing

2326
01:27:24.260 --> 01:27:26.360
that's not nourishing you or the world

2327
01:27:26.360 --> 01:27:28.990
and cut that shit out, do something epic.

2328
01:27:28.990 --> 01:27:32.253
Like, it's not that easy, but it is that easy.

2329
01:27:33.310 --> 01:27:35.460
Yeah, I mean, I think the trick is

2330
01:27:36.780 --> 01:27:40.860
for the person who feels trapped and they're like,

2331
01:27:40.860 --> 01:27:43.430
well, that's great, but like, I can't do that, you know,

2332
01:27:43.430 --> 01:27:44.741
it's about that first step.

2333
01:27:44.741 --> 01:27:46.994
Like, what's that first letter that you're writing

2334
01:27:46.994 --> 01:27:49.500
when you're writing your story, just write one letter.

2335
01:27:49.500 --> 01:27:50.333
Right, start with something.

2336
01:27:50.333 --> 01:27:51.594
You have to start with something.

2337
01:27:51.594 --> 01:27:53.387
Surround yourself with inspiration.

2338
01:27:53.387 --> 01:27:54.677
And you don't need to know what your legacy is.

2339
01:27:54.677 --> 01:27:57.171
You know what I mean? You just have to follow a thread.

2340
01:27:57.171 --> 01:27:59.629
Like, I'm feeling pulled in the direction

2341
01:27:59.629 --> 01:28:02.380
of trying to learn how to play the drums or whatever it is.

2342
01:28:02.380 --> 01:28:03.510
It could be the stupidest thing in the world, you know?

2343
01:28:03.510 --> 01:28:05.640
And I think there's a lot of embarrassment

2344
01:28:05.640 --> 01:28:06.473
around that kind of stuff.

2345
01:28:06.473 --> 01:28:08.803
It's like, these are juvenile aspirations

2346
01:28:08.803 --> 01:28:12.071
that we've been told, you know, we should sort of push aside

2347
01:28:12.071 --> 01:28:15.485
and get into the business of being responsible adults.

2348
01:28:15.485 --> 01:28:18.860
Well, I think the process of reinvention,

2349
01:28:18.860 --> 01:28:20.461
first of all, I think it should happen always.

2350
01:28:20.461 --> 01:28:25.461
But secondly, I think especially as adults,

2351
01:28:26.350 --> 01:28:29.480
we feel like, well, I already wrote my story.

2352
01:28:29.480 --> 01:28:31.097
I'm already this, and I'm already that,

2353
01:28:31.097 --> 01:28:32.327
and I'm already a dad, and I'm already-

2354
01:28:32.327 --> 01:28:33.160
This is who I am.

2355
01:28:33.160 --> 01:28:35.080
I'm already like, this is me.

2356
01:28:35.080 --> 01:28:37.798
But like you're evolving every day.

2357
01:28:37.798 --> 01:28:40.780
Like, literally on a cellular level, you're always evolving.

2358
01:28:40.780 --> 01:28:44.510
So, like take your body's cue and just bring it up here,

2359
01:28:44.510 --> 01:28:49.510
and, you know, allow yourself the freedom to not know.

2360
01:28:51.010 --> 01:28:56.010
And when we go back to thinking why we pursue certain things

2361
01:28:58.360 --> 01:29:03.360
as kids, like, it's just because you're like that seems fun.

2362
01:29:04.169 --> 01:29:05.598
Yeah, there's nothing else going on

2363
01:29:05.598 --> 01:29:06.806
other than that, right?

2364
01:29:06.806 --> 01:29:08.389
You know?

2365
01:29:09.570 --> 01:29:11.140
Don't follow somebody else's script.

2366
01:29:11.140 --> 01:29:14.160
Like, go take the cooking class, go take the drum lessons.

2367
01:29:14.160 --> 01:29:16.004
Like, somebody was in my class the other day,

2368
01:29:16.004 --> 01:29:17.351
and she was like, God, I always really wanted

2369
01:29:17.351 --> 01:29:18.309
to take a ballet class.

2370
01:29:18.309 --> 01:29:19.830
I was like, do it.

2371
01:29:19.830 --> 01:29:21.170
Like, who cares?

2372
01:29:21.170 --> 01:29:23.088
Yeah, you're probably never gonna be in the New York Ballet

2373
01:29:23.088 --> 01:29:26.723
at like X years old, but why not?

2374
01:29:26.723 --> 01:29:27.770
Just fricking do it.

2375
01:29:27.770 --> 01:29:28.854
You don't know what that's gonna open up.

2376
01:29:28.854 --> 01:29:31.862
Like, maybe you're meant to be the PR person for that.

2377
01:29:31.862 --> 01:29:34.250
You know, it's like, you just don't know

2378
01:29:34.250 --> 01:29:35.552
how the dominoes are gonna fall.

2379
01:29:35.552 --> 01:29:36.798
And you shouldn't necessarily

2380
01:29:36.798 --> 01:29:37.881
even try to understand that.

2381
01:29:37.881 --> 01:29:38.810
You know what I mean?

2382
01:29:38.810 --> 01:29:41.080
And I think that what happens is

2383
01:29:41.080 --> 01:29:43.540
whether it's your story of going from corporate lawyer

2384
01:29:43.540 --> 01:29:45.340
to the things that you're doing now,

2385
01:29:45.340 --> 01:29:49.720
or, you know, Dan Harris going from having a panic attack

2386
01:29:49.720 --> 01:29:52.580
on national television in front of five million people

2387
01:29:52.580 --> 01:29:54.773
to being like this advocate for meditation.

2388
01:29:55.810 --> 01:29:59.130
The sort of media narrative is to compress that and to-

2389
01:29:59.130 --> 01:30:01.124
It's like a 30 second, yeah.

2390
01:30:01.124 --> 01:30:02.195
This just happened overnight.

2391
01:30:02.195 --> 01:30:05.070
Like, you were a corporate lawyer,

2392
01:30:05.070 --> 01:30:05.903
and you snapped your fingers

2393
01:30:05.903 --> 01:30:08.130
and now you're like doing all this amazing stuff.

2394
01:30:08.130 --> 01:30:10.560
Or, you know, I lost 50 pounds in two minutes

2395
01:30:10.560 --> 01:30:12.775
and ran Ultraman like a week later, you know?

2396
01:30:12.775 --> 01:30:14.780
And it's like, when you look at the timeline,

2397
01:30:14.780 --> 01:30:16.738
like I was talking about this with Dan,

2398
01:30:16.738 --> 01:30:18.685
he had his panic attack in 2004,

2399
01:30:18.685 --> 01:30:21.880
and he didn't do his first five minute meditation

2400
01:30:21.880 --> 01:30:23.117
until 2009.

2401
01:30:23.117 --> 01:30:24.660
That's five years later.

2402
01:30:24.660 --> 01:30:28.322
So, it's like the neatly packaged narrative that the media,

2403
01:30:28.322 --> 01:30:31.540
you know, is shoving down your throat isn't actually real.

2404
01:30:31.540 --> 01:30:33.460
You know, this is like hard work.

2405
01:30:33.460 --> 01:30:36.578
And it's like, you know, throwing pebbles in the pond

2406
01:30:36.578 --> 01:30:39.950
and putting your toe in the water and then slipping

2407
01:30:39.950 --> 01:30:41.910
and doing a million things that are embarrassing

2408
01:30:41.910 --> 01:30:44.880
and messing up and failing and not knowing what you're doing

2409
01:30:44.880 --> 01:30:46.890
and questioning yourself and all of these things.

2410
01:30:46.890 --> 01:30:49.560
But all of those things are not failures.

2411
01:30:49.560 --> 01:30:50.393
They're successes

2412
01:30:50.393 --> 01:30:53.257
because they're just steps along this journey, you know?

2413
01:30:53.257 --> 01:30:55.074
And so, I think the important thing is to embark

2414
01:30:55.074 --> 01:30:56.110
on the journey,

2415
01:30:56.110 --> 01:30:58.712
to have the courage to embrace the not knowing.

2416
01:30:58.712 --> 01:31:00.729
And it doesn't have to be about goal setting

2417
01:31:00.729 --> 01:31:02.820
or achieving some objective.

2418
01:31:02.820 --> 01:31:04.972
It's about just embracing something different and new,

2419
01:31:04.972 --> 01:31:06.805
something that might be intimidating

2420
01:31:06.805 --> 01:31:09.520
but you're inexplicably drawn to,

2421
01:31:09.520 --> 01:31:13.040
and just not trying to understand the why behind it.

2422
01:31:13.040 --> 01:31:16.409
Yeah, and I think almost all of my lessons

2423
01:31:16.409 --> 01:31:18.770
in the past few years have been in retrospect.

2424
01:31:18.770 --> 01:31:19.820
Like, it's not like

2425
01:31:19.820 --> 01:31:24.820
in the moment I'm necessarily understanding the poignancy

2426
01:31:25.461 --> 01:31:27.669
of something in that time.

2427
01:31:27.669 --> 01:31:30.150
I think it's the reflection that's important.

2428
01:31:30.150 --> 01:31:34.491
You look back on it and it seems to create this,

2429
01:31:34.491 --> 01:31:37.840
you can create, you can write your story retrospectively

2430
01:31:37.840 --> 01:31:38.750
by looking backwards

2431
01:31:38.750 --> 01:31:41.111
and going, oh, this makes perfect sense, of course.

2432
01:31:41.111 --> 01:31:41.944
You know what I mean?

2433
01:31:41.944 --> 01:31:44.750
Like, you know, Dan Harris got assigned

2434
01:31:44.750 --> 01:31:47.639
to cover spirituality and religion, you know,

2435
01:31:47.639 --> 01:31:50.300
he didn't want to, you know, and that set him on a,

2436
01:31:50.300 --> 01:31:52.150
he didn't know why he was doing that then.

2437
01:31:52.150 --> 01:31:53.346
You look back now and it's like, oh, like,

2438
01:31:53.346 --> 01:31:56.041
that's something that a novelist would've fabricated

2439
01:31:56.041 --> 01:31:58.833
for a book, you know, like that doesn't just happen.

2440
01:31:58.833 --> 01:32:01.791
And that's happened to me many times,

2441
01:32:01.791 --> 01:32:03.070
these weird things that have happened.

2442
01:32:03.070 --> 01:32:04.373
I'm sure you've experienced that.

2443
01:32:04.373 --> 01:32:05.206
Yeah, absolutely.

2444
01:32:05.206 --> 01:32:06.910
And then you can write the narrative

2445
01:32:06.910 --> 01:32:08.260
like backwards like that.

2446
01:32:08.260 --> 01:32:10.205
But when it's happening, it doesn't make sense.

2447
01:32:10.205 --> 01:32:11.413
You don't understand it.

2448
01:32:11.413 --> 01:32:14.422
And I think it's about being okay with that.

2449
01:32:14.422 --> 01:32:15.380
Yeah, you do.

2450
01:32:15.380 --> 01:32:20.380
And I think that being okay with that discomfort,

2451
01:32:21.281 --> 01:32:23.560
like a lot of life is uncomfortable.

2452
01:32:23.560 --> 01:32:25.800
It's not always gonna be like...

2453
01:32:28.040 --> 01:32:31.540
I do believe in celebrating little victories, 100%,

2454
01:32:31.540 --> 01:32:36.137
but most of the time, if you have any type of momentum,

2455
01:32:36.137 --> 01:32:37.560
you're gonna have discomfort,

2456
01:32:37.560 --> 01:32:38.706
a lot of discomfort along the way.

2457
01:32:38.706 --> 01:32:40.570
Well, discomfort is the crucible for growth

2458
01:32:40.570 --> 01:32:41.490
and change anyway.

2459
01:32:41.490 --> 01:32:43.451
Like, you should welcome the discomfort.

2460
01:32:43.451 --> 01:32:45.593
If you're uncomfortable, then something's happening.

2461
01:32:45.593 --> 01:32:46.701
Yeah.

2462
01:32:46.701 --> 01:32:48.230
You know?

2463
01:32:48.230 --> 01:32:49.820
I mean, I feel that all the time.

2464
01:32:49.820 --> 01:32:54.712
And I think people, like you mentioned about Dan Harris,

2465
01:32:54.712 --> 01:32:57.940
or even people's like success stories, you know,

2466
01:32:57.940 --> 01:33:00.450
you look at people, whatever Warren Buffet,

2467
01:33:00.450 --> 01:33:03.703
like these icons in Western society.

2468
01:33:03.703 --> 01:33:05.780
And when you, you know, when you take down,

2469
01:33:05.780 --> 01:33:07.350
you break down those stories, you know,

2470
01:33:07.350 --> 01:33:09.450
whether in their books or whatever,

2471
01:33:09.450 --> 01:33:12.370
you're like, damn, that was a lot of hustle.

2472
01:33:12.370 --> 01:33:14.320
Like, that was a lot of little stepping zones

2473
01:33:14.320 --> 01:33:15.749
and a lot of seeds planted.

2474
01:33:15.749 --> 01:33:19.900
Like, I say like plant a whole field of seeds.

2475
01:33:19.900 --> 01:33:21.130
You don't know what's gonna sprout.

2476
01:33:21.130 --> 01:33:23.769
Like, those connections might not even happen

2477
01:33:23.769 --> 01:33:25.727
until decades later.

2478
01:33:25.727 --> 01:33:28.930
And it is in retrospect that sort of the synchronicity

2479
01:33:28.930 --> 01:33:31.130
of things kind of pop up, and you're like, whoa,

2480
01:33:31.130 --> 01:33:34.410
like that was absolutely supposed to happen for me.

2481
01:33:34.410 --> 01:33:35.243
Right.

2482
01:33:35.243 --> 01:33:37.458
And it's about not judging, not making judgment calls

2483
01:33:37.458 --> 01:33:40.210
on the good or the bad of whatever is happening.

2484
01:33:40.210 --> 01:33:42.130
Like, right before, right when you walked in,

2485
01:33:42.130 --> 01:33:44.179
we were talking before we started recording,

2486
01:33:44.179 --> 01:33:46.760
and you were supposed to go to LA and do this TV show.

2487
01:33:46.760 --> 01:33:48.530
Yep, in three days.

2488
01:33:48.530 --> 01:33:50.690
On social media, you were getting all ready to go,

2489
01:33:50.690 --> 01:33:52.160
it's an exciting thing.

2490
01:33:52.160 --> 01:33:55.302
And then at the last minute, the plug got pulled.

2491
01:33:55.302 --> 01:33:57.340
So, what happened?

2492
01:33:57.340 --> 01:33:58.734
So I was supposed to,

2493
01:33:58.734 --> 01:34:00.160
I'm still so frustrated about this!

2494
01:34:00.160 --> 01:34:02.011
Literally the executive producer called me like an hour ago,

2495
01:34:02.011 --> 01:34:04.950
I was supposed to be on this show with NBC

2496
01:34:04.950 --> 01:34:07.870
and Esquire Network where it was called "Enter the Wild,"

2497
01:34:07.870 --> 01:34:10.850
and we were gonna literally live in the wild for a month

2498
01:34:10.850 --> 01:34:15.850
with this survival tracker, professional guy, Tom Brown.

2499
01:34:16.960 --> 01:34:19.680
And apparently he pulled out from the show.

2500
01:34:19.680 --> 01:34:20.513
And now it's-

2501
01:34:20.513 --> 01:34:21.573
Three days before.

2502
01:34:21.573 --> 01:34:22.510
Three days.

2503
01:34:22.510 --> 01:34:23.614
I mean, this was like,

2504
01:34:23.614 --> 01:34:26.349
if anyone can imagine taking a month away from your life,

2505
01:34:26.349 --> 01:34:31.050
literally off the grid, no business calls, no family calls,

2506
01:34:31.050 --> 01:34:31.883
nothing.

2507
01:34:31.883 --> 01:34:32.716
Yeah, you gotta get ready for that.

2508
01:34:32.716 --> 01:34:33.549
I mean, that is like-

2509
01:34:33.549 --> 01:34:35.215
A lot of prep.
That is everything.

2510
01:34:35.215 --> 01:34:38.340
And I work with, because I have such like a,

2511
01:34:38.340 --> 01:34:40.140
my work is like with a lot of different brands

2512
01:34:40.140 --> 01:34:41.690
and a lot of different studios.

2513
01:34:41.690 --> 01:34:45.020
It's not like I tell one boss and I take a leave, you know?

2514
01:34:45.020 --> 01:34:49.394
I had to like to contact like 20 people to get their okay

2515
01:34:49.394 --> 01:34:51.110
or just to give them notice.

2516
01:34:51.110 --> 01:34:53.606
So, yes, to say that that was like,

2517
01:34:53.606 --> 01:34:55.430
I mean, that was a blow, man.

2518
01:34:55.430 --> 01:34:56.655
And just that call.

2519
01:34:56.655 --> 01:34:58.310
And I was so excited about it.

2520
01:34:58.310 --> 01:35:00.490
I was excited about like doing this amazing thing.

2521
01:35:00.490 --> 01:35:02.106
I was like, this is my next adventure.

2522
01:35:02.106 --> 01:35:04.660
Like, this is, you know, you kind of,

2523
01:35:04.660 --> 01:35:06.420
I'm in that place where I'm like, what's next?

2524
01:35:06.420 --> 01:35:07.280
Like, I want something else.

2525
01:35:07.280 --> 01:35:08.113
I want something else.

2526
01:35:08.113 --> 01:35:09.863
And the driven part of me is like, yeah,

2527
01:35:09.863 --> 01:35:11.120
that's your challenge.

2528
01:35:11.120 --> 01:35:12.654
You're gonna be some warrior chick in the woods.

2529
01:35:12.654 --> 01:35:14.170
(Robin laughs)

2530
01:35:14.170 --> 01:35:18.140
And yeah, it looks like very likely is not gonna happen now.

2531
01:35:18.140 --> 01:35:19.130
And...

2532
01:35:20.420 --> 01:35:21.253
But it's-

2533
01:35:21.253 --> 01:35:22.868
And that's creating space, but I know,

2534
01:35:22.868 --> 01:35:25.993
well enough to know that is creating space

2535
01:35:25.993 --> 01:35:28.407
for whatever it is I am supposed to be doing.

2536
01:35:28.407 --> 01:35:29.240
Yeah, I mean,

2537
01:35:29.240 --> 01:35:30.260
the next thing I came outta your mouth was like,

2538
01:35:30.260 --> 01:35:32.329
well, there obviously is something better,

2539
01:35:32.329 --> 01:35:33.162
or I wasn't supposed to do that.

2540
01:35:33.162 --> 01:35:35.570
It sort of, you were talking about planting a ton of seeds.

2541
01:35:35.570 --> 01:35:36.956
Well, that was a seed that germinated

2542
01:35:36.956 --> 01:35:39.599
and was starting to grow and was about to sprout

2543
01:35:39.599 --> 01:35:41.160
and the lawn mower just ran it over.

2544
01:35:41.160 --> 01:35:42.406
So, it's like, all right, well,

2545
01:35:42.406 --> 01:35:44.430
what other seeds look like they're popping up over here

2546
01:35:44.430 --> 01:35:46.430
and let's just go over there.

2547
01:35:46.430 --> 01:35:48.346
Yeah, and like, you know,

2548
01:35:48.346 --> 01:35:50.690
my sister's going to Thailand next week from India,

2549
01:35:50.690 --> 01:35:52.216
and I'm like, maybe I should be going to Thailand.

2550
01:35:52.216 --> 01:35:54.217
You know, I mean, it's just like that's how my mind works.

2551
01:35:54.217 --> 01:35:59.217
And it's only through falling flat on my face so many times

2552
01:36:00.910 --> 01:36:05.910
and wallowing does nothing.

2553
01:36:05.940 --> 01:36:06.891
It does nothing.

2554
01:36:06.891 --> 01:36:08.650
Not to say you shouldn't let like those,

2555
01:36:08.650 --> 01:36:09.483
just the disappointment.

2556
01:36:09.483 --> 01:36:11.990
You should feel it, you know, you should feel how you feel.

2557
01:36:11.990 --> 01:36:15.193
But just gotta move on to the next thing.

2558
01:36:16.040 --> 01:36:16.948
How was India?

2559
01:36:16.948 --> 01:36:18.923
God, I can't wait to go back.

2560
01:36:18.923 --> 01:36:20.676
My sister's living there for another year,

2561
01:36:20.676 --> 01:36:23.885
so I will have an opportunity to be there again.

2562
01:36:23.885 --> 01:36:25.100
Where were you in India?

2563
01:36:25.100 --> 01:36:27.170
I was in...

2564
01:36:27.170 --> 01:36:28.365
My sister was living in Delhi at the time.

2565
01:36:28.365 --> 01:36:30.140
So, I was in Delhi and then we went to Agra

2566
01:36:30.140 --> 01:36:31.853
where the Taj Mahal was, which is,

2567
01:36:31.853 --> 01:36:33.660
have you been to Taj Mahal?

2568
01:36:33.660 --> 01:36:34.493
Never been.

2569
01:36:34.493 --> 01:36:35.830
It is like just as impressive

2570
01:36:35.830 --> 01:36:36.690
as you'd think it'd be.

2571
01:36:36.690 --> 01:36:39.492
Like, gasp worthy.

2572
01:36:39.492 --> 01:36:40.751
That will never become a cliche.

2573
01:36:40.751 --> 01:36:44.143
Like, Taj Mahal is as epic as it should be.

2574
01:36:45.070 --> 01:36:46.140
So, we went to Taj Mahal.

2575
01:36:46.140 --> 01:36:48.296
Then we went to Rishikesh, which is an amazing area,

2576
01:36:48.296 --> 01:36:50.040
which is kind of like,

2577
01:36:50.040 --> 01:36:51.640
it's where The Beatles learned to meditate.

2578
01:36:51.640 --> 01:36:53.965
It's kind of like dubbed as the birthplace-

2579
01:36:53.965 --> 01:36:55.173
Lots of ashrams.

2580
01:36:55.173 --> 01:36:56.690
Yeah, lots of ashrams.

2581
01:36:56.690 --> 01:36:58.260
Birthplace of meditation, all that.

2582
01:36:58.260 --> 01:37:00.589
And that was amazing.

2583
01:37:00.589 --> 01:37:05.589
Like, so many people searching,

2584
01:37:06.700 --> 01:37:08.046
like I have never been in a place

2585
01:37:08.046 --> 01:37:13.046
where you just felt like people were seeking and craving

2586
01:37:14.220 --> 01:37:19.220
and wanting like answers, like with a capital A, Answers.

2587
01:37:20.600 --> 01:37:22.881
And that was really like weird and neat.

2588
01:37:22.881 --> 01:37:27.160
I kind of thought it'd be like this zen yoga place

2589
01:37:27.160 --> 01:37:29.770
and there's tons of yoga, but not like Western yoga.

2590
01:37:29.770 --> 01:37:30.740
Very hatha yoga.

2591
01:37:30.740 --> 01:37:32.730
Very, very different types of yoga than I'm used to,

2592
01:37:32.730 --> 01:37:34.183
which is a vinyasa practice.

2593
01:37:36.036 --> 01:37:40.060
But what was so interesting to me was not the yoga at all.

2594
01:37:40.060 --> 01:37:43.780
It was more like the interactions with like travelers

2595
01:37:43.780 --> 01:37:46.743
and these like just nomadic kind of folks

2596
01:37:46.743 --> 01:37:51.196
who find themselves at like a satsang in this random,

2597
01:37:51.196 --> 01:37:52.685
you know, it was like this Brazilian,

2598
01:37:52.685 --> 01:37:55.500
picture of this like Brazilian guru

2599
01:37:55.500 --> 01:37:59.150
and 100 random people just like chanting.

2600
01:37:59.150 --> 01:38:01.500
And I'm like, and they're all very visually different,

2601
01:38:01.500 --> 01:38:03.340
all colors, all ages.

2602
01:38:03.340 --> 01:38:05.630
And I'm just like, we all are here.

2603
01:38:05.630 --> 01:38:07.373
Like, it was just, it was a weird,

2604
01:38:08.638 --> 01:38:12.060
you know, it's rare that I have these like moments

2605
01:38:12.060 --> 01:38:13.490
to just give me absolute pause.

2606
01:38:13.490 --> 01:38:16.330
And I'm like, this is like, we're all here

2607
01:38:16.330 --> 01:38:17.710
in this time space continuum.

2608
01:38:17.710 --> 01:38:18.543
I don't know.

2609
01:38:18.543 --> 01:38:20.514
It was like this really like weird snapshot

2610
01:38:21.734 --> 01:38:26.097
that I recognized as a snapshot, which is also rare.

2611
01:38:26.097 --> 01:38:27.180
So, but so India was amazing.

2612
01:38:27.180 --> 01:38:30.605
Rishikesh was just such a trip and beautiful

2613
01:38:30.605 --> 01:38:33.043
and right at the foot of the Himalayas.

2614
01:38:33.970 --> 01:38:36.839
Goa, we also spent some time in Goa, Southern beaches.

2615
01:38:36.839 --> 01:38:39.270
That was amazing.

2616
01:38:39.270 --> 01:38:42.929
We were in, oh my God, what was the name of the beach?

2617
01:38:42.929 --> 01:38:45.100
The most Southern beach.

2618
01:38:45.100 --> 01:38:46.595
Totally forgetting now.

2619
01:38:46.595 --> 01:38:47.928
Goa is pretty south too, isn't it?

2620
01:38:47.928 --> 01:38:51.174
No, yeah, so Goa is the most Southern state,

2621
01:38:51.174 --> 01:38:54.295
but then the most Southern beach in Goa is where we were,

2622
01:38:54.295 --> 01:38:56.430
and I can not believe I'm forgetting the name,

2623
01:38:56.430 --> 01:38:57.700
maybe that's good thing

2624
01:38:57.700 --> 01:38:59.160
'cause I don't want it to get overcrowded

2625
01:38:59.160 --> 01:39:00.880
'cause it's already getting too touristy.

2626
01:39:00.880 --> 01:39:04.560
But it was such a gift to be able to hang out with my mom

2627
01:39:04.560 --> 01:39:06.470
and my sister. Like, I don't remember the last time we spent

2628
01:39:06.470 --> 01:39:08.220
more than three days together.

2629
01:39:08.220 --> 01:39:11.840
So, I think, you know, I have a tattoo on my arm.

2630
01:39:11.840 --> 01:39:15.210
These are swallows and it's three birds,

2631
01:39:15.210 --> 01:39:19.240
and the middle one is my mom, the bottom one is my sister,

2632
01:39:19.240 --> 01:39:20.230
the top one is me.

2633
01:39:20.230 --> 01:39:22.887
And it just kind of honors all our journeys.

2634
01:39:22.887 --> 01:39:26.408
And my mom is a real example of reinvention.

2635
01:39:26.408 --> 01:39:28.060
She's a Cuban refugee

2636
01:39:28.060 --> 01:39:31.350
and taught herself English watching "Sesame Street" and PBS

2637
01:39:31.350 --> 01:39:32.183
at 16.

2638
01:39:32.183 --> 01:39:34.117
And then, you know, eight years later was,

2639
01:39:34.117 --> 01:39:35.580
you know, in medical school.

2640
01:39:35.580 --> 01:39:38.517
So, she's a really inspiring story for me.

2641
01:39:38.517 --> 01:39:40.770
And then my sister, even though she's my little sister,

2642
01:39:40.770 --> 01:39:42.997
is way wiser than I probably will ever be.

2643
01:39:42.997 --> 01:39:45.390
And is now living in India and working for an NGO

2644
01:39:45.390 --> 01:39:46.970
and just literally changing the world.

2645
01:39:46.970 --> 01:39:50.482
So, I have, I'm sort of bookended by these amazing women

2646
01:39:50.482 --> 01:39:52.963
who inspire me in very, very unique ways,

2647
01:39:52.963 --> 01:39:55.785
and it wasn't lost on me that we were

2648
01:39:55.785 --> 01:39:59.082
kind of like all three of us on this beach in India

2649
01:39:59.082 --> 01:40:00.692
and I managed to take a month off,

2650
01:40:00.692 --> 01:40:04.467
and it was just like very, I was like, okay, sunshine,

2651
01:40:04.467 --> 01:40:06.590
this is it, like literally in a bottle.

2652
01:40:06.590 --> 01:40:07.423
So, it was great.

2653
01:40:07.423 --> 01:40:08.501
That's really cool.

2654
01:40:08.501 --> 01:40:11.330
What do you think about this sort of dearth

2655
01:40:11.330 --> 01:40:13.480
of female role models?

2656
01:40:13.480 --> 01:40:16.037
You know, we're in the world of the Kardashians

2657
01:40:16.037 --> 01:40:17.686
and, you know, "The Bachelorette,"

2658
01:40:17.686 --> 01:40:18.910
and all this sort of thing.

2659
01:40:18.910 --> 01:40:20.350
And there just really aren't, you know,

2660
01:40:20.350 --> 01:40:22.850
we're in a crisis when it comes to that.

2661
01:40:22.850 --> 01:40:24.020
It's a huge crisis.

2662
01:40:24.020 --> 01:40:24.853
I mean, I mean, thank-

2663
01:40:24.853 --> 01:40:25.939
And it's not that there aren't amazing women doing

2664
01:40:25.939 --> 01:40:29.020
incredibly inspiring things all over the place,

2665
01:40:29.020 --> 01:40:31.804
but it's a misdirect when it comes to media.

2666
01:40:31.804 --> 01:40:33.673
The most readily available images.

2667
01:40:33.673 --> 01:40:35.000
Absolutely.

2668
01:40:35.000 --> 01:40:37.987
I mean, I'm lucky and your kids are lucky

2669
01:40:37.987 --> 01:40:40.420
because they're surrounded by, you know, their mom,

2670
01:40:40.420 --> 01:40:43.790
but I was, I think it...

2671
01:40:43.790 --> 01:40:47.320
Unfortunately it's starting from the closest images

2672
01:40:47.320 --> 01:40:51.710
that you have to you for a lot of girls.

2673
01:40:51.710 --> 01:40:54.080
And I don't know how to change it.

2674
01:40:54.080 --> 01:40:56.500
I mean, I would like to think like that is really one

2675
01:40:56.500 --> 01:40:57.715
of the things I wanna do with Undo.

2676
01:40:57.715 --> 01:40:59.875
You know, we sort of feature these vignettes

2677
01:40:59.875 --> 01:41:03.994
and empowering stories of real people doing awesome stuff.

2678
01:41:03.994 --> 01:41:07.220
And we wanna encourage people to tell their stories

2679
01:41:07.220 --> 01:41:10.243
and be visible and vocal about the stuff that they're doing

2680
01:41:10.243 --> 01:41:11.240
'cause then, you know,

2681
01:41:11.240 --> 01:41:13.600
that sort of raises the consciousness a little bit

2682
01:41:13.600 --> 01:41:15.249
and the dialogue.

2683
01:41:15.249 --> 01:41:18.480
But, you know, in terms of like crazy celebrity culture

2684
01:41:18.480 --> 01:41:22.220
and the rabbit hole of like Us Weekly,

2685
01:41:22.220 --> 01:41:24.321
which I will definitely buy on a plane

2686
01:41:24.321 --> 01:41:26.410
and, you know, whatever.

2687
01:41:26.410 --> 01:41:28.380
I've done it, I admit it.

2688
01:41:28.380 --> 01:41:31.050
But, you know, I'm also old enough to know.

2689
01:41:31.050 --> 01:41:33.710
You know, it's like, would I give that to like a little kid

2690
01:41:33.710 --> 01:41:36.530
and show that as an example of what to be in the world?

2691
01:41:36.530 --> 01:41:37.363
Absolutely not.

2692
01:41:37.363 --> 01:41:39.893
So, I don't know, man.
But I think that,

2693
01:41:39.893 --> 01:41:41.983
I think that there is a mass yearning for that.

2694
01:41:41.983 --> 01:41:43.619
It's not, it's not an isolated thing.

2695
01:41:43.619 --> 01:41:45.305
So, it seems like it could be something

2696
01:41:45.305 --> 01:41:48.668
that you could weave into the brand consultancy work

2697
01:41:48.668 --> 01:41:49.840
that you do.

2698
01:41:49.840 --> 01:41:52.671
Like, to be able to, you know, sort of, you know,

2699
01:41:52.671 --> 01:41:56.045
raise the pedestal of maybe some women

2700
01:41:56.045 --> 01:41:57.039
that you haven't heard of

2701
01:41:57.039 --> 01:41:59.754
and put them, you know, in an ad for a brand

2702
01:41:59.754 --> 01:42:03.652
in a way that's aspirational for young girls, you know?

2703
01:42:03.652 --> 01:42:05.566
There has to be opportunities for that

2704
01:42:05.566 --> 01:42:07.649
to work within the system.

2705
01:42:07.649 --> 01:42:09.153
And there are really good organizations

2706
01:42:09.153 --> 01:42:10.520
that I've worked with.

2707
01:42:10.520 --> 01:42:12.373
Like, I Am That Girl is kind of,

2708
01:42:13.300 --> 01:42:14.980
the messaging behind it is really cool.

2709
01:42:14.980 --> 01:42:17.480
It's kind of like, I am that girl who's,

2710
01:42:17.480 --> 01:42:22.480
it's basically getting accomplished people to be like,

2711
01:42:22.550 --> 01:42:26.090
yeah, I am that girl who was made fun of for running,

2712
01:42:26.090 --> 01:42:27.423
which I literally was.

2713
01:42:27.423 --> 01:42:28.890
I was made fun of by the way for how I ran

2714
01:42:28.890 --> 01:42:31.598
when I was in sixth grade.

2715
01:42:31.598 --> 01:42:32.900
You know, little things like that

2716
01:42:32.900 --> 01:42:35.903
to sort of like spark dialogue and inspire folks.

2717
01:42:35.903 --> 01:42:37.093
But I think from like,

2718
01:42:38.080 --> 01:42:40.070
from an aesthetic perspective,

2719
01:42:40.070 --> 01:42:41.880
from like a stylized perspective,

2720
01:42:41.880 --> 01:42:44.490
it's really important to sort of bring people

2721
01:42:44.490 --> 01:42:46.350
from like the fray and bring them in.

2722
01:42:46.350 --> 01:42:47.183
And I feel like

2723
01:42:47.183 --> 01:42:50.490
that's sort of what the running crew community did

2724
01:42:50.490 --> 01:42:51.323
for runners.

2725
01:42:51.323 --> 01:42:53.963
And I think that it's something

2726
01:42:53.963 --> 01:42:56.550
that the wellness movement can now do for women.

2727
01:42:56.550 --> 01:42:59.440
Like, I think that it is absolutely a beacon of,

2728
01:42:59.440 --> 01:43:01.680
I think the wellness movement is a beacon of light for women

2729
01:43:01.680 --> 01:43:03.210
and in a really healthy way,

2730
01:43:03.210 --> 01:43:05.680
not in a way that's focused on body image,

2731
01:43:05.680 --> 01:43:07.541
but in a way that's focused on empowerment

2732
01:43:07.541 --> 01:43:09.143
in a very different,

2733
01:43:09.143 --> 01:43:12.233
it's a different type of feminism I think honestly.

2734
01:43:13.080 --> 01:43:14.130
Interesting.

2735
01:43:15.000 --> 01:43:16.510
We gotta wrap it up here in a couple minutes,

2736
01:43:16.510 --> 01:43:19.264
but I did want to ask you about your experience going up

2737
01:43:19.264 --> 01:43:23.390
to Vermont and working with the Love Your Brain guys

2738
01:43:23.390 --> 01:43:25.780
with Kevin.
I love those guys!

2739
01:43:25.780 --> 01:43:26.914
So when we were in Vegas,

2740
01:43:26.914 --> 01:43:31.228
we had that great honor of meeting the Pearce brothers.

2741
01:43:31.228 --> 01:43:33.284
And Kevin Pearce, for those who don't know,

2742
01:43:33.284 --> 01:43:36.030
was a incredible snowboarder

2743
01:43:36.030 --> 01:43:38.511
who was well on his way to the Olympics

2744
01:43:38.511 --> 01:43:41.111
and potentially beating Shaun White.

2745
01:43:41.111 --> 01:43:45.240
And he had a massive head injury shortly before,

2746
01:43:45.240 --> 01:43:46.548
two Olympics ago, right?

2747
01:43:46.548 --> 01:43:51.548
And it's been a long road back for him with his health,

2748
01:43:53.350 --> 01:43:54.900
and he's doing great now.

2749
01:43:54.900 --> 01:43:56.270
There was an amazing documentary made

2750
01:43:56.270 --> 01:43:58.627
about his life called "The Crash Reel," which was on HBO.

2751
01:43:58.627 --> 01:44:00.780
I don't know if it's still airing.

2752
01:44:00.780 --> 01:44:02.680
But if you have a chance to see this documentary,

2753
01:44:02.680 --> 01:44:03.513
it's extraordinary.

2754
01:44:03.513 --> 01:44:05.124
And Robin and I both had the great opportunity

2755
01:44:05.124 --> 01:44:08.810
to meet these guys at this event.

2756
01:44:08.810 --> 01:44:09.709
And it was really touching.

2757
01:44:09.709 --> 01:44:11.763
You know, they're special guys for sure.

2758
01:44:11.763 --> 01:44:12.663
Very special guys.

2759
01:44:12.663 --> 01:44:15.620
And you, and they put together,

2760
01:44:15.620 --> 01:44:18.575
so they have a nonprofit called Love Your Brain.

2761
01:44:18.575 --> 01:44:22.810
And they were putting together a group of people to do the,

2762
01:44:22.810 --> 01:44:24.383
this, the Burlington marathon,

2763
01:44:24.383 --> 01:44:26.610
and you wanted to coach them and get them ready.

2764
01:44:26.610 --> 01:44:28.840
So, I wanna hear about that.
It was so fun.

2765
01:44:28.840 --> 01:44:30.373
I mean, gosh, that was,

2766
01:44:30.373 --> 01:44:33.720
so Adam and Kevin reached out to me to coach the team

2767
01:44:33.720 --> 01:44:38.000
and Love Your Brain had a lot of different participants

2768
01:44:38.000 --> 01:44:38.833
on the team.

2769
01:44:38.833 --> 01:44:40.890
So, the Burlington marathon allows people to participate

2770
01:44:40.890 --> 01:44:42.233
in the marathon as a relay,

2771
01:44:42.233 --> 01:44:44.133
as a half, or as a full distance.

2772
01:44:44.133 --> 01:44:46.833
So, people kind of pick the distances they were capable

2773
01:44:46.833 --> 01:44:47.791
of running,

2774
01:44:47.791 --> 01:44:52.017
and Will Halby has, his family has a farm

2775
01:44:52.017 --> 01:44:56.590
in Vermont called, oh my God...

2776
01:44:57.470 --> 01:44:58.900
I can't believe I'm forgetting the name of this either.

2777
01:44:58.900 --> 01:45:01.053
My brain. So, they have a farm in Vermont.

2778
01:45:01.053 --> 01:45:02.980
And I'm so sorry I forget the name.

2779
01:45:02.980 --> 01:45:07.980
But they host us and they have this like amazing community

2780
01:45:11.510 --> 01:45:14.675
that they basically allow nonprofits to come

2781
01:45:14.675 --> 01:45:16.950
and, you know, host their events there.

2782
01:45:16.950 --> 01:45:18.241
The pictures looked incredible.

2783
01:45:18.241 --> 01:45:19.560
It's stunning.

2784
01:45:19.560 --> 01:45:21.720
And for me, like speaking of nature from Newark, that was-

2785
01:45:21.720 --> 01:45:23.650
Yeah, being an urban girl.

2786
01:45:23.650 --> 01:45:24.483
That, I mean, I was like,

2787
01:45:24.483 --> 01:45:26.328
oh, I really needed this air.

2788
01:45:26.328 --> 01:45:28.010
I needed this greenery.

2789
01:45:28.010 --> 01:45:30.739
And, you know, like truly on a cellular level, I needed it.

2790
01:45:30.739 --> 01:45:35.140
So, I basically helped coach the 70 person team.

2791
01:45:35.140 --> 01:45:37.130
And, you know, some people were running 5Ks,

2792
01:45:37.130 --> 01:45:38.165
some people were running 10Ks,

2793
01:45:38.165 --> 01:45:40.400
a few people were running the full,

2794
01:45:40.400 --> 01:45:42.110
bunch of people running the half.

2795
01:45:42.110 --> 01:45:43.647
And we spent five days out there,

2796
01:45:43.647 --> 01:45:46.500
and it was so much more than a marathon.

2797
01:45:46.500 --> 01:45:47.951
Like, I got there on a Thursday,

2798
01:45:47.951 --> 01:45:52.770
and we had like a storytelling circle

2799
01:45:52.770 --> 01:45:55.329
where you have traumatic brain injury victims talking about,

2800
01:45:55.329 --> 01:45:59.364
you know, one girl was thrown off a horse

2801
01:45:59.364 --> 01:46:02.028
and like, she's, you know, basically,

2802
01:46:02.028 --> 01:46:03.983
you know, wheelchair bound.

2803
01:46:06.382 --> 01:46:08.410
She walked a mile of, you know,

2804
01:46:08.410 --> 01:46:10.677
assisted, she walked a mile of the marathon.

2805
01:46:10.677 --> 01:46:15.677
So, little things like that are not so little when you make,

2806
